TRIPS=[]
def gear(name,spec,why,query='',kind='confirm'):
    return {'name':name,'specification':spec,'why':why,'query':query,'decision':kind}
def trip(id,title,place,country,region,water,habitat,landscape,methods,access,climate,fish,summary,evidence,season,decisions,kit,mistakes,questions,related=()):
    TRIPS.append(dict(id=id,title=title,place=place,country=country,region=region,water=water,habitats=habitat,landscape=landscape,methods=methods,access=access,climate=climate,fish=fish,summary=summary,evidence=[{'source':a,'claim':b} for a,b in evidence],season=season,sections=[{'title':a,'text':b} for a,b in decisions],kit=kit,mistakes=mistakes,questions=questions,related=list(related),review={'status':'source-checked','checked':DATE,'fieldTested':False,'independentExpertReviewed':False,'automatedAudit':'see research/quality-report.json'}))

trip('situk-coho-river','Situk River coho fishing: bank, float and tackle decisions','Yakutat / Situk River','United States','North America','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Coastal temperate'],['Fly','Lure / spinning'],['Bank','River float'],'cool',['Coho salmon'],
'A coho trip needs a river-reach decision before a rod recommendation. Separate bank access, a full float and the condition of the fish you want to target.',
[('situk','ADF&G describes streamer and leech flies, jigs, spinners and spoons for Situk coho. It distinguishes fresher lower-river fish from dark or spawning fish farther upstream, particularly later in September. Its described float from Nine Mile Bridge to the Lower Landing is 14 miles.')],
'Plan around the coho run and the intended river reach, not an undifferentiated “Alaska salmon season.” The source makes the later-September distinction important. This page does not establish current run strength, water level or a daily catch probability.',
[('Choose a day you can actually fish','Compare a bank outing with a float as different products. For a float, establish the pickup and takeout arrangement before choosing accommodation or estimating available fishing time. For a bank day, identify the legal access and the length of the walk. A guide who supplies the boat does not necessarily supply waders, boots or transport back to your car.'),
('Assemble around presentation weight','Choose whether you will cast lures, fish jigs or use fly tackle. Ask for the actual lure or fly sizes and the weight needed for the intended water. Those answers determine the rod’s casting range and the line system. An existing suitable salmon setup is more useful than an arbitrary “Alaska combo.” Pack replacement leaders and a small working selection rather than every salmon lure in a catalog.'),
('Keep a separate boat-day plan','A Yakutat halibut day is not an extension of this river list. Keep wet wading equipment separate from dry clothing and boat footwear, and ask whether changing or drying space is available. Put the equipment you need at the first stop in accessible luggage. If the river is not suitable on the planned day, a guide-confirmed alternative is a contingency, not a guaranteed substitute fishery.')],
[gear('Coho casting or fly setup','Match the actual presentation weight and local leader choice.','A spinner, jig and fly line do not share one rod-rating system.'),gear('Wading system','Boot fit, belt and suitable traction; only if the actual outing involves wading.','A bank or float booking may have different footwear requirements.','breathable fishing waders rubber sole boots'),gear('Leader and lure replenishment','Obtain a short local list of types, sizes and quantities.','A few exact replacements beat a generic assortment.','salmon fishing leader tackle organizer'),gear('Dry change and rain shell','Pack a complete dry layer set away from used river gear.','The return journey should not depend on wet clothing drying immediately.','waterproof roll top dry bag','consider')],
['Do not treat darker upstream fish and fresh lower-river arrivals as identical objectives.','Do not turn a 14-mile float description into a casual walk-and-wade itinerary.'],
['Which reach and launch/takeout will we use?','Are waders, boots, rods and transport all included?','Which lure weights or fly setup should I bring?'],['yakutat-halibut','delta-clearwater-grayling'])

trip('yakutat-halibut','Yakutat halibut charter guide: depth, tackle and boat-day preparation','Yakutat Bay and adjacent Gulf waters','United States','North America','Saltwater',['Reef & bottom','Offshore'],['Subarctic coast'],['Bottom fishing','Jigging'],['Charter boat'],'cool',['Pacific halibut'],
'Build this as a saltwater boat trip, not a cold-weather version of river fishing. The charter’s depth, equipment and weather decisions determine what belongs in your bag.',
[('halibut','ADF&G describes productive Yakutat halibut fishing from late April through September. It notes that winter fishing is possible but harder because of weather and deeper fish, and lists bait and large jigs among approaches.')],
'Late April–September is a documented broad reference, not a guarantee that every date or boat can reach the same water. Do not infer a suitable winter charter merely because halibut are present. Ask about weather alternatives and the operator’s current operating period.',
[('Buy the charter before buying its tackle','Request the actual gear-provided list, including rods, reels, terminal tackle and any fighting support the crew uses. Ask whether you will hold and work a jig or mainly fish bait on the bottom. Those experiences can demand different effort and equipment. Unless there is a specific personal-tackle objective, borrowing the matched boat setup should be the first comparison against a new purchase.'),
('Depth is an equipment input','Have the skipper specify the expected depth and current range before ordering sinkers, jigs or line. A reel’s size number alone is not a usable specification. Capacity, compatible line and the load the crew expects must be considered together. The shopping list should leave those technical items unresolved rather than recommending the same offshore kit to every customer.'),
('Prepare the person as well as the bag','Clarify the length of the boat day, shelter, seating, facilities and footwear. Tell the operator about limits on sustained reeling or lifting so the trip can be matched to you. Keep warm layers and a dry backup accessible. Discuss fish processing and the journey home separately from the charter; neither a cooler nor a fish box automatically solves the entire return itinerary.')],
[gear('Boat-supplied rod/reel','Confirm depth-appropriate matched equipment and what is included.','Avoid paying twice for tackle already supplied.'),gear('Jigs or bait rigs','Exact weights and rig type set by skipper and water.','Bottom-contact requirements cannot be selected from destination alone.'),gear('Deck footwear','Crew-approved grip and deck compatibility.','Wading boots are not automatically suitable on a boat.','non marking fishing deck boots'),gear('Layered boat clothing','Rain shell, insulation and dry storage suited to the forecast.','A long boat day needs accessible rather than buried layers.','fishing waterproof bibs rain jacket','consider')],
['Do not apply a salmon-river rod recommendation to bottom fishing.','Do not describe a winter outing as equivalent to a summer charter.'],
['What depth and fishing method are planned?','What tackle and flotation do you supply?','How are weather changes and fish processing handled?'],['situk-coho-river','norway-skrei'])

trip('delta-clearwater-grayling','Delta Clearwater grayling: clear-water flies, small lures and float access','Delta Clearwater River, Alaska','United States','North America','Freshwater',['Spring-fed streams','Rivers & streams'],['Subarctic'],['Fly','Lure / spinning'],['Bank','River float'],'cool',['Arctic grayling'],
'A small, spring-fed grayling river should not inherit the gear or visibility assumptions of a glacial mainstem. Access and presentation are the central planning choices here.',
[('clearwater','ADF&G identifies the Delta Clearwater as a cold, clear, low-gradient spring-fed stream, with grayling fishing from May to September. It describes dry flies, nymphs and small lures, and notes limited public shore access compared with boat or float access.')],
'May–September is the agency’s grayling reference. Its separate late-September coho opportunity is a different target and should receive its own setup. Confirm access and conditions for your dates rather than assuming the whole river can be fished from a road.',
[('Make access the first filter','Decide whether this is a short bank session around an established access point or a planned float. A float adds transport, takeout, personal flotation and equipment-storage decisions that a simple “small stream” label misses. Do not infer easy shore access from the river’s modest size. Keep the route choice separate from the target-species choice.'),
('Pack two presentations, not two shops','Build a compact dry-fly and subsurface option, or a small-lure selection for spinning. Select the rod and line around that choice instead of reusing a heavy salmon outfit solely because both trips are in Alaska. Ask for the current useful fly sizes or lure weights. Leave capacity in the box for a small local purchase rather than committing the full tackle budget in advance.'),
('Plan for cold water without assuming wading','A boat or accessible bank may remove the need to purchase waders. When wading is part of the actual plan, confirm footwear, depth expectations and dry storage. Keep the fishing kit small enough to manage in the selected craft. The preparation list must include transport and clothing decisions, not just a list of flies.')],
[gear('Light-presentation setup','Fly line and rod matched to chosen flies, or a rod rated for the selected small lures.','Grayling fishing is not automatically a heavy salmon-tackle task.'),gear('Dry and subsurface selection','Use a locally selected fly-size range or small lures.','Two coherent approaches are easier to use than a mixed novelty kit.','small fly fishing box nymph dry flies'),gear('Float-day storage','Compact waterproof bag sized for the craft.','Keep a spare layer out of splash and paddle water.','small waterproof dry bag','consider'),gear('Access and flotation','Confirm route, takeout and appropriate personal flotation.','A float plan needs more than a rod and waders.')],
['Do not describe this spring-fed river as glacier-fed.','Do not market its late-run coho as the same experience as grayling dry-fly fishing.'],
['Where can we legally fish from shore?','What is the float and shuttle arrangement?','Which presentation sizes are working for the chosen target?'],['tanana-glacial-system','soca-fly-fishing'])

trip('tanana-glacial-system','Tanana drainage fishing: glacial mainstem versus clear tributaries','Tanana drainage, Interior Alaska','United States','North America','Freshwater',['Glacier-influenced rivers','Rivers & streams'],['Glacier-influenced','Subarctic'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Bank','Guided boat'],'cool',['Arctic grayling','Northern pike','Burbot'],
'“Glacier fishing” is not one activity. Here the useful distinction is between a glacier-influenced river system and individual clear-water tributaries or connected lakes.',
[('tanana','ADF&G describes a drainage containing both roadside ponds and clear spring-fed streams, with several distinct sport-fish opportunities.'),('tanana-report','An archived ADF&G report dated June 26, 2026 described the Tanana rising with glacial melt as temperatures warmed. This is evidence of a process observed then, not a report for today.')],
'Do not assign the drainage one universal month rating. Select a target and named water, then review its actual water-level trend and access. Seasonal melt, weather and the chosen tributary can matter differently. No current discharge or visibility feed is connected to this guide.',
[('Replace the vague trip description','Ask whether you will fish the mainstem, a tributary mouth, an upstream clear tributary or a separate lake. Those are different habitat and access decisions. A photo of turbid water does not establish that every nearby fishery is turbid. Equally, a clear tributary photograph does not establish a wadeable route through the larger river.'),
('Match the outfit to the target','Do not combine grayling, pike and burbot into a single recommended rod-and-lure kit. Begin with the intended species, presentation and access method. The equipment conversation for casting small flies should be different from one about larger predator lures or fishing bait. Keep this drainage overview as a decision hub and follow a narrower guide before buying technical tackle.'),
('Keep water changes visible in the itinerary','Use an access-led fallback rather than committing all days to one unknown reach. Ask which decisions will be made shortly before departure and how you will receive updates. A supplied satellite communicator or boat does not mean the route is suitable for independent use. Do not describe travel on an actual glacier as a recreational fishing access option.')],
[gear('Target-specific setup','Name the species, exact water and method first.','A drainage-wide fish list is not a compatibility specification.'),gear('Water-resistant day storage','Organize separate wet and dry compartments.','The route may involve transfers between boat and bank.','waterproof fishing backpack','consider'),gear('Route and communications plan','Confirm launch, return route, communications and operator responsibilities.','Remote or changing water requires an explicit plan.'),gear('Local tackle replenishment','Wait for the named-water recommendation.','A generic glacier-fishing kit is not a meaningful product.')],
['Do not confuse glacier-fed water, ice fishing and traveling on a glacier.','Do not display a June report as live September conditions.'],
['Which exact water and target are we booking?','Which recent water-level source do you use?','What is the alternative if the chosen reach is unsuitable?'],['delta-clearwater-grayling','rocky-mountain-alpine'])

trip('rocky-mountain-alpine','Rocky Mountain alpine-lake fishing: verify the fishery before the hike','Rocky Mountain National Park, Colorado','United States','North America','Freshwater',['Alpine lakes'],['Mountain'],['Fly','Lure / spinning'],['Hike-in','Bank'],'cool',['Trout'],
'An alpine fishing plan is a hiking plan with a verified fishery attached. A beautiful lake and a map pin are not enough to justify a long approach carrying tackle.',
[('rocky','The National Park Service notes brown, brook, rainbow and cutthroat trout in Rocky Mountain National Park, but says most high-altitude lakes lack reproducing fish populations because of cold water and limited spawning habitat.')],
'This source does not provide a universal ice-off date or a guarantee that a particular lake holds fish. Verify the selected water, trail and current access separately. Elevation, weather and the actual lake determine the plan; “summer in Colorado” is insufficient.',
[('Choose the water before the gear','Shortlist a named lake with confirmed angling access and fishery information. Then assess the trail distance, ascent, return time and the group’s hiking ability. Decide whether the trip is principally a hike with opportunistic fishing or a dedicated fishing day. That decision should limit the tackle weight you carry.'),
('Trade duplicates for useful alternatives','Select one primary presentation and a compact fallback. A travel rod should be evaluated by packed length and actual casting requirements, not simply marketed as ultralight. Avoid carrying several full-size tackle boxes for an unconfirmed fishery. A small net may be useful, but compare its packed size and handling benefit against the walk before automatically adding it.'),
('Separate shore fishing from wading','Do not turn every trout destination into a waders purchase. For a hike-in bank plan, footwear should first support the approach and the actual shoreline. Set a return-time rule before the first cast. Keep rain protection, navigation, drinking-water planning and a headlamp independent of how optimistic the fishing forecast sounds.')],
[gear('Packable matched fishing setup','Packed length, presentation weight and practical protection.','A compact rod is useful only if it casts the chosen tackle.'),gear('Small tackle or fly box','A selected primary method and fallback.','Reduce carried duplicates before cutting essential outdoor equipment.','compact waterproof fly box','consider'),gear('Daypack and dry layer storage','Capacity for hiking essentials plus protected tackle.','The fishing list must not displace the approach equipment.','lightweight hiking daypack dry bag','consider'),gear('Access and water verification','Named lake, current approach and fish presence.','Avoid a gear-heavy hike to an unsuitable or fishless destination.')],
['Do not infer fish from a scenic lake photograph.','Do not publish a single fixed ice-off month for every elevation.'],
['Is this named lake an established fishery?','What are the current approach and lake conditions?','Can the whole outing be completed without wading?'],['tasmania-great-lake','eg linton-river'.replace(' ','')])

trip('texas-inshore-redfish','Texas inshore redfish and trout: flats, shell and seasonal depth','Texas coastal bays','United States','North America','Brackish',['Estuaries & mangroves','Saltwater flats'],['Coastal'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Skiff','Bank'],'warm',['Red drum','Spotted seatrout'],
'A Texas bay trip needs a habitat choice: grass, shell, channels and open shore do not call for identical presentations or abrasion assumptions.',
[('red-drum','TPWD gives different line-strength examples for smaller red drum around shell or structure versus open grass flats, illustrating why habitat changes tackle choice.'),('seatrout','TPWD describes spotted seatrout in shallow bays and estuaries and a broad shift from shallower spring/summer areas toward deeper water as conditions cool.')],
'Treat the seasonal depth description as a starting point, not a daily location forecast. Name the bay, target and habitat. A warm afternoon in a colder season does not convert the entire trip into a summer flats pattern.',
[('Ask what “inshore” means on this booking','Compare a casting session over shallow grass with fishing near oyster structure or deeper channels. Specify whether the group wants fly fishing, conventional casting or bait. Those choices determine casting practice, equipment and what a successful outing looks like. Do not compare operators solely on the phrase “redfish trip.”'),
('Make the line decision explicit','Request a complete main-line and leader recommendation for the chosen presentation and abrasion environment. Rod power labels and reel-size numbers differ across product families, so a similar-looking replacement should not be accepted automatically. The agency’s illustrative line strengths are not a rule for every braid, fish size or leader material.'),
('Let access control footwear','A skiff day may need only suitable deck footwear; wading adds separate fit, traction and local-hazard decisions. Confirm that wading is actually planned before adding specialized boots. Keep sun coverage and rain protection available together, and consider a short local instruction session when the customer is learning to cast rather than sell equipment as a substitute for practice.')],
[gear('Habitat-matched casting setup','Specify lure weight, target size and cover.','Open grass and abrasive structure are different jobs.'),gear('Main line and leader','Confirm material and abrasion requirements together.','Do not transfer a line-strength number between unrelated systems.'),gear('Sun-coverage clothing','Existing suitable shirt, hat and glasses first.','Keep the shopping list tied to missing personal equipment.','long sleeve fishing sun shirt','consider'),gear('Wading or deck footwear','Choose after access is confirmed.','Do not buy both by default.','fishing deck shoes')],
['Do not label brackish estuary habitat as a freshwater lake.','Do not treat all redfish sizes and cover types as one tackle specification.'],
['Which bay and habitat will we fish?','What casting skill does the selected method need?','Is wading part of the actual outing?'],['keys-flats','andros-bonefish'])

trip('cape-hatteras-surf','Cape Hatteras surf fishing: beach access, rigs and conditions','Cape Hatteras, North Carolina','United States','North America','Saltwater',['Surf & beaches'],['Atlantic coast'],['Bait fishing','Lure / spinning'],['Bank'],'mixed',['Season-dependent coastal fish'],
'Build a beach session around target, casting load and access. A general “saltwater rod” recommendation says too little about what the rig must do in the surf.',
[('hatteras','Cape Hatteras National Seashore distinguishes surf, pier, sound and offshore fishing. Its general visitor guidance identifies spring and fall as strong broad periods and points to offshore fishing in midsummer; these are not species-by-species calendars.')],
'Use the broad spring/fall context to start research, then identify the target fish and recent beach conditions. Do not promise the same species every month or transfer an offshore summer description onto a beach itinerary.',
[('Choose a fishing method before the rod length','Compare casting lures with placing a bait rig beyond nearby wash. The total casting load matters: include the sinker and bait, not just the sinker label. Have a local shop recommend a suitable range for the expected surf, then verify the rod rating. Longer is not automatically better for every angler, access point or presentation.'),
('Pack the beach logistics','Decide how the equipment will reach the fishing area and how far the group can comfortably carry it. A large cart may be helpful on one route and unhelpful on another. Keep a compact working tackle box and a separate replenishment box at your base. Plan how to keep reels and spare tackle out of the sand.'),
('Do not confuse access with safe conditions','An accessible beach is not a statement that entering the water is appropriate. Fish from a suitable shore position and use local advice about the actual conditions. Set a stopping point for worsening weather and an alternative activity instead of treating the first day as a must-fish obligation. A guided introduction can be more valuable than adding another rod.')],
[gear('Surf casting setup','Rod rating must cover the entire intended rig.','Bait plus sinker can exceed a recommendation based on sinker alone.'),gear('Target-specific rig components','Hook, leader and weight selected as a system.','The source does not support one rig for every coastal species.'),gear('Sand-resistant organization','Rod support and closed working box, suitable to the access.','Keep equipment managed rather than laid in wash or sand.','surf fishing rod holder tackle box','consider'),gear('Carry system','Choose capacity after distance and route are known.','Avoid a cart purchase that does not fit the chosen access.','fishing beach cart','optional')],
['Do not copy an offshore gear list onto a surf page.','Do not equate public access with safe wading conditions.'],
['Which target and rig are suitable for these dates?','What total casting weight should the rod handle?','How far is the equipment carry from the access point?'],['keys-offshore','texas-inshore-redfish'])

trip('keys-flats','Florida Keys flats fishing: choose bonefish, permit or tarpon first','Florida Keys flats and backcountry','United States','North America','Saltwater',['Saltwater flats','Estuaries & mangroves'],['Subtropical'],['Fly','Lure / spinning'],['Skiff'],'warm',['Bonefish','Permit','Tarpon'],
'“Flats fishing” describes a setting, not one interchangeable target or outfit. Build the trip around a primary fish and the presentation the angler can use well.',
[('keys-world','The Keys visitor bureau identifies bonefish, permit and tarpon as shallow-water targets and distinguishes these trips from reef, wreck and offshore experiences.')],
'The reviewed visitor page is not a month-by-month species calendar. Dates should remain a local-confirmation step until a target-specific source and operator input support them. Do not advertise a single all-species peak month.',
[('Choose the experience before the grand slam','Ask whether the customer wants instruction, varied action or a concentrated attempt at one difficult target. A three-species wish list can distract from a suitable first day. Compare how many people can actively fish at once, the amount of time standing and whether the guide will use fly or conventional gear.'),
('Treat technique as a separate branch','A fly outfit must match its line and flies; a spinning outfit must match the intended lure or bait presentation. Neither follows automatically from the label “saltwater.” Confirm the complete setup and equipment provision before choosing retail products. Practice the actual presentation where possible instead of expecting a premium reel to compensate for an unfamiliar method.'),
('Make the boat bag smaller','Keep essentials reachable without leaving loose bags and line-catching items around the casting area. Bring the sunglasses you already trust, sun coverage and a rain layer. Ask whether wading is planned before adding boots or waders. Share the preferred pace and experience level with the guide, especially when planning a family trip rather than a specialist fishing day.')],
[gear('Target-specific outfit','Select bonefish, permit or tarpon and method first.','The same flats label does not establish one rod or leader.'),gear('Appropriate line system','Confirm line taper, material and leader with the selected setup.','Avoid buying a compatible-looking but unsuitable fly or conventional line.'),gear('Polarized glasses and retainer','Comfortable fit with a secure retainer.','Use an existing suitable pair before upgrading.','polarized fishing sunglasses retainer','consider'),gear('Compact skiff bag','Limited footprint with organized accessible compartments.','Keep the casting area clear.','small waterproof fishing boat bag','consider')],
['Do not guarantee a grand slam from a species list.','Do not assume flats fishing requires wading.'],
['Which species is the primary target for these dates?','Will we use fly or conventional tackle?','How many anglers can fish at once?'],['andros-bonefish','belize-permit','keys-offshore'])

trip('keys-offshore','Florida Keys offshore fishing: distinguish trolling, reef and wreck trips','Florida Keys offshore waters','United States','North America','Saltwater',['Offshore','Reef & bottom'],['Subtropical'],['Trolling','Bottom fishing','Jigging'],['Charter boat'],'warm',['Sailfish','Mahi-mahi','Tuna'],
'An offshore outing should be sold by target, method and time on the water. Reef fishing, working a wreck and trolling for pelagic fish are not substitute names for the same charter.',
[('keys-world','Florida Keys tourism lists sailfish, mahi-mahi and tuna among offshore targets and presents reef/wreck and light-tackle trips as distinct options.')],
'No exact species calendar or live offshore report is established by this source. Choose the target with the skipper for the selected period. The fact that a species appears on a destination page does not mean it will be a realistic objective on every half-day charter.',
[('Compare the actual fishing time','Ask how much of the outing is travel, how the planned grounds change with conditions, and what the quoted duration includes. Compare a private boat against a shared trip and check how the group participates. The customer choosing a hands-on casting or jigging day may not want a predominantly trolling itinerary.'),
('Prefer matched boat equipment initially','Ask what the crew supplies for the chosen method and whether bringing personal equipment is useful. Trolling, deep bottom fishing and casting can have different line, reel and rod needs. Do not buy one heavy offshore combo and assume it covers all three. Pack only the personal technical items the operator agrees will be used.'),
('Plan the weather alternative honestly','Ask whether a change of target or fishing area is possible and what changes the price or experience. Do not silently replace an offshore booking with an inshore activity that the customer did not choose. Keep personal layers, drinking-water arrangements, storage and deck footwear in the preparation list, alongside the operator’s contact and departure details.')],
[gear('Crew-matched tackle','Inventory tied to trolling, jigging or bottom fishing.','Avoid unnecessary duplicate rods and reels.'),gear('Personal fishing tools','Ask which tools are supplied and safe for passenger use.','Heavy rigging and fish handling may be crew tasks.'),gear('Deck clothing and footwear','Footwear approved for the vessel; sun and rain layers.','A boat list is not a surf-fishing or wading list.','non marking fishing deck shoes'),gear('Personal dry pouch','Capacity for phone and small essentials rather than a huge bag.','Keep deck space manageable for the group.','waterproof phone pouch dry bag','consider')],
['Do not promise offshore species on the basis of a generic four-hour trip.','Do not label reef, wreck and trolling tactics as identical.'],
['Which method and target does this price cover?','How much time is normally spent traveling?','What happens if the offshore plan changes?'],['yakutat-halibut','quepos-offshore','belize-reef'])

trip('andros-bonefish','Andros bonefishing: flats access, sight-casting and a lighter gear list','Andros, The Bahamas','Bahamas','North America','Saltwater',['Saltwater flats','Estuaries & mangroves'],['Tropical island'],['Fly'],['Skiff','Guided wading'],'warm',['Bonefish'],
'Choose a part of Andros and a guide-led fishing style before assembling the bag. The goal is a useful sight-fishing plan, not a generic Caribbean tackle bundle.',
[('andros','The Bahamas tourism board describes Andros as a network of sandy flats, mangrove cays, creeks and channels supporting bonefish, permit and tarpon. Its account emphasizes spotting fish and following guide direction.')],
'The source does not establish a complete bonefish calendar or specific tide window for every Andros area. Keep arrival airport, lodge location and fishing area aligned, and have the guide explain how the intended dates affect daily access and presentation.',
[('Confirm the actual island base','Andros is not one interchangeable marina. Put the lodge or guide’s meeting location and transfer arrangement into the itinerary before buying flights. Ask whether fishing begins from that base or requires an additional transfer. Include a practical arrival buffer rather than treating the first hour after landing as guaranteed fishing time.'),
('Prepare to see and present','A well-organized practice session can be more useful than extra flies. Ask which casting distances and conditions are realistic for the outing and practice with the outfit you intend to use. Confirm the fly selection locally. Separate a bonefish setup from any specialist permit or tarpon additions rather than assuming one outfit meets every possible encounter.'),
('Choose footwear from the planned access','A skiff-only day and guided wading across a particular substrate require different footwear decisions. Ask for the operator’s exact recommendation and whether rental options exist. Reduce loose gear around the casting position. Use a small accessible bag for sun coverage, rain protection and spare leaders, with a separate dry compartment for travel essentials.')],
[gear('Bonefish fly outfit','Guide-confirmed rod, reel, tropical line and leader combination.','An ordinary trout line is not automatically the intended saltwater line.'),gear('Locally selected flies','Pattern type, size, weight and useful quantities.','A large mixed saltwater set may omit the useful variants.'),gear('Flats footwear, if needed','Exact substrate, fit and guided-wading plan.','Do not buy wading footwear for a skiff-only booking.','saltwater flats wading boots'),gear('Sun kit and line-safe storage','Glasses, retainer, hat and a compact soft bag.','Keep the casting area free from loose equipment.','fishing sun hat polarized glasses retainer','consider')],
['Do not treat every Andros airport and lodge as interchangeable.','Do not sell a three-species equipment package when the customer booked bonefish.'],
['Which part of Andros is the fishing base?','Will we wade, fish from a skiff, or both?','Which line and weighted fly variants should I bring?'],['keys-flats','belize-permit','cosmoledo-gt'])

trip('belize-permit','Belize permit and flats trips: choose the base, target and casting plan','Placencia, Punta Gorda and Belize flats','Belize','North America','Saltwater',['Saltwater flats'],['Tropical coast','Atoll'],['Fly'],['Skiff','Guided wading'],'warm',['Permit','Bonefish','Tarpon'],
'Build a Belize flats trip around a primary target and a practical base. A permit-focused week and a mixed introductory day should not have the same expectations or shopping list.',
[('belize','Belize Tourism Board identifies flats around Ambergris Caye, Turneffe, Placencia and Punta Gorda and names bonefish, tarpon and permit as flats targets.'),('blue-horizon','Its Blue Horizon Lodge listing describes a southern Belize flats operation in the South Water Caye area. This is an operator lead, not an Atlas partner or availability feed.')],
'This research does not support one best month for all Belize flats, targets and bases. Ask the operator to connect your chosen dates to the primary fish, wind exposure and practical access. Avoid treating a grand-slam description as a success forecast.',
[('Pick a base that matches the fishing','Compare transfer requirements, fishing departure point and non-fishing plans. Do not select a hotel in one area while assuming a guide in another can provide an uncomplicated pickup. For a lodge package, clarify which transfers, fishing days and personal equipment are included. For an independent hotel stay, compare the full transport-and-guide cost.'),
('Set a useful definition of success','Ask whether the group values instruction, variety or a concentrated permit attempt. A guide should know the customer’s casting experience before choosing an itinerary. Keep specialist outfits conditional on an actual target rather than selling one outfit for each fish mentioned on a tourism page. Confirm whether a suitable backup setup is available locally.'),
('Separate flats from reef add-ons','A reef day has different tackle and participation requirements. Make it an explicit alternate experience, not an automatic replacement for a permit day. The preparation list should preserve common personal items while changing technical equipment. A small number of well-chosen flies and leaders is preferable to buying a broad saltwater collection without the guide’s input.')],
[gear('Primary-target fly system','Rod, reel, line and leader accepted by the guide.','Permit, bonefish and tarpon are separate equipment decisions.'),gear('Flies with specified weights','Order exact sizes and weighting after local review.','Pattern names alone can hide important variants.'),gear('Skiff or wading footwear','Select against the itinerary, not the country label.','Keep unnecessary wading purchases off a boat-only trip.','saltwater flats wading boots'),gear('Light personal weather kit','Sun coverage, rain shell and protected essentials.','Pack for the actual day and transfers.','lightweight fishing rain jacket','consider')],
['Do not count three target species as a promised grand slam.','Do not switch a flats guide to a reef trip without making the difference explicit.'],
['Which base minimizes transfers to the intended flats?','What should a realistic day look like for my experience level?','Which exact fly variants should I purchase?'],['andros-bonefish','belize-reef','keys-flats'])

trip('belize-reef','Belize reef fishing: a different trip from flats or deep sea','Belize Barrier Reef area','Belize','North America','Saltwater',['Reef & bottom'],['Tropical reef'],['Bottom fishing','Lure / spinning'],['Charter boat'],'warm',['Snapper','Grouper','Barracuda'],
'Compare reef trips by grounds, method and customer participation rather than assuming all Belize boat fishing is the same experience.',
[('belize','Belize Tourism Board distinguishes reef fishing for species including snapper, grouper and barracuda from flats fishing and from pelagic trips beyond the reef.')],
'The reviewed overview establishes trip types, not current catches or an exact species season. Ask the captain which targets and grounds are appropriate for the dates and sea conditions. Keep the reef plan separate from a deep-sea promise.',
[('Make the comparison concrete','Ask whether the trip is private or shared, how many people actively fish and which techniques are used. A family introduction and a specialist target session need different pacing. Clarify whether snorkeling or other activities are part of the advertised time so that comparisons are based on fishing time rather than the overall excursion length.'),
('Do not buy an offshore outfit by default','Have the operator describe the tackle already on board. Actual depth, current and target determine the useful personal equipment. A flats fly rod or a large trolling reel is not automatically a suitable reef purchase. Treat rods, terminal tackle and handling equipment as crew-confirmed items; fill personal-clothing gaps first.'),
('Plan transfers and return needs','Confirm the marina or pickup location rather than assuming every coastal hotel is served. Ask what storage, shade and facilities the boat provides. Keep the day bag compact and have a clear plan for any agreed fish handling or processing. An excursion’s promotional photos should not define what you expect to keep or catch.')],
[gear('Boat-matched reef tackle','Confirm supplied equipment for actual depth and method.','Buy only a requested personal addition.'),gear('Leader and terminal items','Exact crew specification before purchase.','Species lists do not define one hook or leader size.'),gear('Suitable deck footwear','Check grip, fit and operator preferences.','Wading soles are not the default.','non marking fishing deck shoes'),gear('Small sun-and-rain bag','Personal water arrangements, clothing and dry pouch.','Avoid a large hard case that occupies working deck space.','small waterproof boat bag','consider')],
['Do not use “reef” and “deep sea” as interchangeable booking filters.','Do not translate promotional catch photographs into guaranteed species.'],
['Which grounds and method does the trip cover?','How many anglers fish at once?','What gear and personal items are supplied?'],['belize-permit','keys-offshore'])

trip('quepos-offshore','Quepos offshore sportfishing: select the target, boat and actual fishing time','Quepos, Central Pacific coast','Costa Rica','North America','Saltwater',['Offshore'],['Tropical Pacific'],['Trolling'],['Charter boat'],'warm',['Sailfish','Marlin'],
'A Quepos boat-day plan should separate offshore billfish objectives from nearshore alternatives and personal travel logistics.',
[('costa-seasons','Costa Rica’s tourism board highlights Quepos sportfishing during the Central Pacific dry-season discussion and lists sailfish and marlin among regional targets. It also mentions roosterfish, which should not be folded into an offshore billfish promise.')],
'The official dry-season context is a planning lead, not an exact catch calendar for every target. Ask the captain to explain the intended species and grounds for your week. Rain season, boat operation and fish availability are separate questions.',
[('Choose an offshore objective','State whether the goal is a first billfish experience, group participation or a specialist method. Ask how rods are managed and how anglers take turns. A headline fish photograph says little about the experience between strikes. Compare the boat’s facilities, departure point and duration along with the target.'),
('Let the crew own the rigging plan','For a fully equipped charter, ask what personal tackle would actually be useful. Keep rod, reel, line and terminal decisions with the intended technique, rather than importing a reef or inshore setup. Purchase clothing, eye protection and dry storage only after checking what you already have; a charter booking is not a reason to rebuild your entire tackle inventory.'),
('Design a credible alternative','Ask whether a different fishing area or target can be offered if the offshore plan becomes unsuitable. Record what that changes about price and customer expectations. Do not market nearshore roosterfish as the same activity as offshore billfish trolling. Leave room in the travel itinerary for transfers and any agreed weather flexibility.')],
[gear('Charter-provided fishing system','Request inclusions by method, not just “gear included.”','Offshore billfish equipment should be matched as a complete system.'),gear('Personal deck clothing','Light coverage plus accessible weather shell.','Pack for the boat and transfer, not only the hotel.','lightweight fishing sun shirt','consider'),gear('Footwear and dry pouch','Operator-approved footwear with compact protected storage.','Keep deck participation practical.','waterproof phone pouch','consider'),gear('Physical participation plan','Ask about standing, harness use and rotation.','Choose a suitable experience before paying for equipment.')],
['Do not combine roosterfish and billfish into one guaranteed offshore day.','Do not publish a precise peak month without a target-specific source.'],
['What is the primary target for our dates?','How do anglers rotate during the day?','What changes if the offshore grounds are unsuitable?'],['keys-offshore','costa-caribbean-canals'])

trip('costa-caribbean-canals','Costa Rica Caribbean canal fishing: tarpon, snook and a separate coast','Tortuguero and Barra del Colorado area','Costa Rica','North America','Brackish',['Estuaries & mangroves','Rivers & streams'],['Tropical lowland'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Guided boat'],'warm',['Tarpon','Snook'],
'The Caribbean canal experience is not a copy of Costa Rica’s Pacific offshore fishery. Identify the exact water, boat route and target before planning equipment.',
[('costa-caribbean','Costa Rica’s tourism material identifies the Tortuguero and Barra del Colorado canal areas with tarpon and snook opportunities. This supports a distinct Caribbean-waterway guide, not a Pacific-coast season transfer.')],
'No target-specific monthly calendar has been established from this source. Do not reuse Quepos dry-season language for these waterways. Ask how current rain, water levels and the actual route affect the selected fishing dates.',
[('Name the water and route','Clarify whether the trip stays inside waterways, works a river-mouth area or includes another environment. These choices affect comfort, conditions and the useful boat. A destination-wide species list is not evidence that every canal supports the same opportunity. Save the actual transfer and meeting arrangement with the plan.'),
('Select a target-sized system','An outfit for a particular tarpon objective and a snook presentation near cover may not be interchangeable. Request the complete rod, reel, line and leader setup along with the intended lure or fly. Avoid accepting reel-size labels as the only specification. Confirm rentals before packing a long rod tube on a multi-transfer itinerary.'),
('Plan for a guided boat day','Do not add a wading option solely because the route is described as a river or canal. Keep equipment, footwear and access within the operator’s actual plan. Use dry storage for personal items, keep bags manageable for transfers and ask what the day’s facilities and return timing will be. The useful shopping list is mostly the missing personal kit until tackle provision is clear.')],
[gear('Target-specific rod and reel','Establish target size and presentation before choosing ratings.','Avoid a generic “tropical river” combo.'),gear('Leader and presentation kit','Exact guide-approved line and terminal system.','Water type alone does not establish abrasion or shock requirements.'),gear('Transfer-friendly dry bag','Soft compact luggage with accessible essentials.','Match bag dimensions to the actual transfers.','waterproof roll top duffel bag','consider'),gear('Sun, rain and insect planning','Ask the lodge for a current personal-preparation list.','Do not assume conditions are identical to the Pacific coast.','lightweight long sleeve fishing clothing','consider')],
['Do not reuse a Pacific-coast calendar for the Caribbean canals.','Do not recommend independent wading from a generic river classification.'],
['Which named water and route will we fish?','Is all fishing from the boat?','Which target and complete tackle system fit these dates?'],['quepos-offshore','rio-negro-peacock'])

trip('rio-negro-peacock','Rio Negro peacock bass: basin, water level and lure-work planning','Rio Negro programs, Brazilian Amazon','Brazil','South America','Freshwater',['Tropical floodplains','Rivers & streams'],['Tropical floodplain'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Guided boat'],'warm',['Peacock bass'],
'Choose the basin and program before choosing the week. “Amazon fishing” is too broad to justify one season chart, species list or tackle basket.',
[('amazon','Amazon Xtreme Fishing offers Rio Negro peacock-bass programs using both fixed accommodation and moving mothership formats. Its public offering says fishing gear can be supplied, so personal tackle purchases should depend on the exact package.'),('amazon-planner','The operator’s planning library separates water levels, tackle and travel preparation, rather than presenting one undifferentiated Amazon season.')],
'This guide does not assign one low-water month to the entire Amazon. Ask for the operating area and the operator’s assessment for the selected week. A calendar for a different tributary should not be silently substituted for the Rio Negro.',
[('Compare the fishing base','Ask how a fixed lodge and a moving base change daily travel and access to the intended fishing area. Compare usable fishing time, boat arrangements and transfers rather than accommodation photographs alone. A remote journey also needs a clear baggage allowance and a stated contingency for a missed connection; do not assume every transfer can be rescheduled independently.'),
('Match tackle to sustained casting','Describe the customer’s preferred method and tolerance for repeated casting and lure work. Have the guide name the exact lure weights, hook and split-ring requirements, leader arrangement and useful alternatives. A few approved robust combinations are more practical than a large assortment of unverified lures. Do not infer a fish’s scientific identity or maximum size from the generic label “peacock bass.”'),
('Separate personal kit from supplied tackle','Get the equipment inventory in writing for the actual departure. Confirm whether rods, reels, lures and replacements are all covered or only some of them. Keep bag weight focused on what is not supplied, accessible rain protection and dry storage. Treat operator claims about easy catches or insect conditions as marketing, not guaranteed trip facts.')],
[gear('Matched casting outfit','Guide-approved casting range and line system.','Repeated use and lure weight matter more than a generic bass label.'),gear('Lure hardware and replacements','Confirm hooks, split rings, leader and spare quantities together.','An apparently similar lure may have unsuitable components.'),gear('Compact tool kit','Confirm tools provided before carrying duplicates.','Avoid specialized handling purchases without instruction.','fishing split ring pliers','optional'),gear('Transfer-compatible luggage','Use the actual soft-bag size and weight allowance.','Remote transfers can set the practical packing limit.','waterproof soft duffel travel bag','consider')],
['Do not give all Amazon tributaries the same low-water calendar.','Do not repeat promotional claims that insects or difficult fishing are absent.'],
['Which basin and operating area does our week use?','What tackle, replacements and transfers are included?','Which lure weights and baggage limits should we plan around?'],['lower-zambezi-tiger','top-end-barramundi'])

trip('northern-patagonia-trout','Northern Patagonia trout: river floats, spring creeks and lake days','Northern Patagonia, Argentina','Argentina','South America','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams','Spring-fed streams','Lakes & reservoirs'],['Mountain','Lake-fed'],['Fly'],['River float','Bank','Guided boat'],'cool',['Brown trout','Rainbow trout','Brook trout'],
'Build a varied trout itinerary by water type, not by copying one fly selection across every day. A spring creek, river float and lake session are different experiences.',
[('patagonia','SET distinguishes northern Patagonian river floats, slower spring creeks and lake fishing. It describes a December–January dragonfly-related lake opportunity and names the Aluminé, Collón Curá, Chimehuín and Caleufú among its river programs.'),('set-regions','SET’s northern Patagonia offering includes dry-fly, streamer, float and lake options. These are operator-described opportunities, not a complete guide to all Patagonia.')],
'The December–January lake reference belongs to this operator’s northern Patagonia description. Do not apply it to every river, altitude or Chilean destination. Use Southern Hemisphere dates explicitly and obtain the intended water-by-water itinerary before specifying tackle.',
[('Design the week as distinct fishing days','Ask how many days are spent floating, wading spring creeks or fishing lakes and whether the plan changes with conditions. Choose variety deliberately rather than adding transfers for their own sake. A customer prioritizing sight-fishing should not discover that most days are long floats; one wanting less walking should state that before the itinerary is assembled.'),
('Use line and fly systems by job','Ask whether the primary plan is dry-fly presentation, nymphing or streamer work. Match the outfit to that task and define the fallback explicitly. A spare spool or second supplied outfit may be more useful than a new rod for every destination name. Let the guide recommend fly sizes and line choices for the actual waters rather than shopping from a generic Patagonia assortment.'),
('Plan the lodge-to-water logistics','Separate overnight luggage from the boat or walk-day bag. Confirm drying space, vehicle transfers and the equipment provided on each type of day. Camp-based floats add a different baggage and comfort decision from returning to a lodge. The same personal rain and insulation system can support several days, while technical tackle changes with the fishing.')],
[gear('Primary trout fly system','Choose around dry flies, nymphs or streamers.','One region includes several presentation jobs.'),gear('Secondary line or supplied outfit','Confirm the actual lake or streamer requirement.','Avoid buying an unnecessary full second setup.'),gear('Wading kit only for wading days','Size, fit and operator provision checked in advance.','A float or lake day does not automatically require personal waders.','breathable waders rubber sole boots'),gear('Compact day bag and layer storage','Separate daily access from lodge or camp luggage.','Keep changing and drying practical.','waterproof fly fishing day bag','consider')],
['Do not present all Patagonia as one river and one season.','Do not convert a lake hatch reference into a guaranteed daily hatch.'],
['Which waters and methods make up the proposed week?','Which equipment is supplied for each day type?','Do we return to a lodge or move between camps?'],['tasmania-great-lake','mataura-trout','soca-fly-fishing'])

trip('norway-skrei','Northern Norway winter cod: skrei, boat equipment and weather flexibility','Northern Norway coast','Norway','Europe','Saltwater',['Offshore','Reef & bottom'],['Cold coast'],['Jigging','Bottom fishing'],['Charter boat'],'cold',['Atlantic cod / skrei'],
'Winter cod is its own trip category. It should not inherit the clothing, daylight or operating assumptions of a summer fjord holiday.',
[('norway','Visit Norway describes sea fishing across the year, identifies the winter skrei period around January–March, and notes that winter weather calls for experience or a professional guide.')],
'January–March is a broad published skrei reference. It is not evidence that every coastal town, boat and date offers the same opportunity. Keep weather flexibility and the actual operator’s season in the booking conversation.',
[('Choose an operator and working area','Compare the launch location, travel to the fishing grounds and the type of boat day offered. A summer boat-rental listing is not proof of a suitable independent winter trip. Ask what the operator does when conditions change and how a multi-day visit can use alternative days. Choose the experience before deciding how many personal rods to transport.'),
('Make cold-weather provision specific','Ask exactly which flotation clothing, outer layers and boots are supplied and which sizes are available. Personal base layers and gloves may remain your responsibility even where an outer suit is included. Check that all layers fit together with the supplied equipment. Do not describe a warm jacket alone as the complete solution for a cold marine environment.'),
('Select tackle with depth and method','Ask whether the planned fishing is primarily jigging or bait fishing and what weights and line system are used. Match the whole setup rather than selecting an oversized reel as insurance. Confirm which tackle and handling tasks the crew owns. Keep spare gloves and dry layers accessible, and establish how photographs and fish handling will be managed without disrupting safe deck work.')],
[gear('Crew-approved cold-water clothing','Flotation equipment and insulation are separate checks.','Confirm what the operator supplies and exact fit.'),gear('Layered gloves and spare dry pair','Choose dexterity and warmth around the actual task.','Spare dry handwear can be more useful than another lure.','waterproof insulated fishing gloves','consider'),gear('Depth-matched rod and reel','Confirm jig weights, line and expected loads with the skipper.','A summer light-tackle setup may not fit the planned day.'),gear('Protected dry clothing','Keep a separate bag for dry layers.','Do not rely on damp equipment drying during the outing.','waterproof dry bag','consider')],
['Do not equate summer rental-boat availability with winter suitability.','Do not promise fixed weather or catches within a broad seasonal window.'],
['Which winter grounds and method are planned?','Which clothing and flotation equipment are provided?','How are weather days handled across the visit?'],['norway-summer-sea','yakutat-halibut'])

trip('norway-summer-sea','Norway summer sea fishing: guided boat versus self-drive camp','Norwegian coastal fishing camps','Norway','Europe','Saltwater',['Reef & bottom','Offshore'],['Temperate coast'],['Jigging','Bottom fishing'],['Guided boat'],'cool',['Cod','Halibut','Haddock'],
'A coastal fishing camp, a guided charter and a self-drive boat rental are different products. Choose the responsibility level before building the shopping list.',
[('norway','Visit Norway describes long summer fishing days and coastal camps that may offer accommodation, boats or equipment. Those inclusions are possibilities, not a standard entitlement at every property.')],
'Summer light does not establish suitable sea conditions or a specific target at every base. Ask which species and areas fit the actual dates. A summer plan should still have a weather-aware daily operating decision rather than an unlimited-hours assumption.',
[('Choose how much responsibility you want','Compare guided fishing with the experience, navigation and local operating knowledge required by a rental arrangement. This guide does not turn a rental booking into a qualification to operate offshore. For a first visit, request a guided introduction or choose a fully guided service. Match the base to the group rather than to the largest fish in a promotional photograph.'),
('Get an itemized inclusion list','Ask whether boat hire, fuel, electronics, tackle, flotation, clothing and cleaning arrangements are included or separately charged. Compare the total experience instead of a cabin price against a charter price. Check equipment condition and sizes with the operator and confirm who is responsible for daily weather decisions.'),
('Separate the tackle from the camp bag','Identify the actual methods and depths before packing jigs, sinkers and spare line. Heavy duplicate equipment can consume baggage allowance without adding useful options. Keep a day kit organized around the selected boat and leave replenishment gear at the base. Personal rain protection and insulation should still be accessible even on a trip advertised around summer.')],
[gear('Fishing system for selected grounds','Guide specifies depth, line and presentation weight.','Avoid a single Norway-wide rod recommendation.'),gear('Boat and navigation provision','Clarify what is supplied and who operates it.','A gear checklist does not establish boating competence.'),gear('Compact replenishment kit','Bring exact useful replacements after inclusion checks.','Avoid transporting heavy duplicate terminal tackle.'),gear('Marine-weather clothing','Rain shell, layers and deck footwear suited to the forecast.','Long daylight does not remove exposure.','waterproof fishing rain suit','consider')],
['Do not combine a self-drive rental and fully guided charter under one booking promise.','Do not automatically add boat equipment already included in the camp.'],
['What is included beyond accommodation?','Who makes the daily boat and weather decision?','Which methods and depths are appropriate here?'],['norway-skrei','tasmania-great-lake'])

trip('finland-ice-fishing','Finnish lake ice fishing: guided introduction and a rental-first kit','Lake Iso-Virmas experience, Finland','Finland','Europe','Freshwater',['Ice fishing','Lakes & reservoirs'],['Winter'],['Ice jigging'],['Guided ice'],'cold',['Locally confirmed lake fish'],
'Ice fishing is a surface-condition and access category, not “glacier fishing.” Start with a locally operated outing and confirm the full equipment and clothing plan.',
[('finland','Visit Finland lists a provider-run “Ice fishing with a Finn” experience at Lake Iso-Virmas. That is evidence of a particular guided activity, not proof of suitable ice on any date or at every Finnish lake.')],
'There is no safe-ice date derived from a month selector. Book only an outing the local provider can assess and operate under the actual conditions. This guide deliberately does not publish a universal ice-thickness rule or a guaranteed winter operating window.',
[('Rent the unfamiliar system first','For an introductory visit, compare the supplied rod, bait or jig, auger and other equipment with buying a personal kit. A guide may prepare the fishing position, but the inclusions must be explicit. Ask how long the session lasts, how far you move and what warm-up options exist. Select a shorter introduction when the group is unsure about comfort.'),
('Build clothing around stillness','Do not simply copy the clothing you wear while exercising outdoors. Describe the planned duration and activity level to the provider and ask what thermal layers and boots are needed. Confirm any rental clothing by size and availability. Keep spare dry gloves separate from working gloves and make the personal bag manageable for the access method.'),
('Keep access assessment with the local operator','Never translate a scenic frozen-lake image into independent access advice. Ask what conditions cause the activity to be changed or canceled and where the alternative takes place. The plan should show that status before departure. A purchased auger or flotation garment does not establish that an unknown lake is safe to enter.')],
[gear('Guided ice-fishing tackle','Confirm rod, jig/bait, auger and seating provision.','Renting first can avoid a one-trip purchase.'),gear('Thermal boots and clothing','Provider-confirmed fit for session length and conditions.','A winter coat alone may not meet the whole requirement.','insulated winter boots thermal base layers'),gear('Spare dry handwear','Keep an accessible backup pair.','Protect comfort when handling wet tackle.','insulated winter gloves','consider'),gear('Local access assessment','Provider makes the actual ice and route decision.','No shopping item replaces it.')],
['Do not confuse a frozen lake with a glacier.','Do not infer safe access from the calendar.'],
['Is the activity operating under the present ice conditions?','Which clothing sizes and fishing equipment are provided?','What are the warm-up and cancellation arrangements?'],['norway-skrei','rocky-mountain-alpine'])

trip('soca-fly-fishing','Soča Valley fly fishing: reach choice, trout and presentation','Soča Valley','Slovenia','Europe','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Mountain'],['Fly'],['Bank','Guided wading'],'cool',['Marble trout','Grayling','Rainbow trout'],
'A Soča plan should identify a specific reach and target. Alpine scenery is not a reason to copy an Alaska glacial-river kit or a generic trout assortment.',
[('soca','Soča Valley tourism identifies marble trout, grayling and rainbow trout among its fly-fishing targets and distinguishes areas and management sections within the river system.')],
'The source lists different target and section dates, so this guide does not collapse them into one universal season. Choose the actual reach and primary target for your dates. We do not classify the modern river as glacier-fed merely because it is alpine or turquoise.',
[('Pick the reach and fishing objective','Ask whether the trip emphasizes general trout fishing, a larger-fish search or instruction. Specify walking and wading preferences. An operator can then compare access and presentation choices instead of assuming every customer wants the same technical challenge. Keep the exact meeting point and fishing section with the plan.'),
('Build a presentation-led fly selection','Ask which dry, subsurface or streamer approach fits the selected water and dates. Different line and leader decisions may follow. Avoid ordering a full range of fly patterns without sizes or weighting. A smaller locally selected box leaves more room for useful spare leaders and makes it easier for a visiting angler to understand why each item is included.'),
('Keep handling and equipment practical','Confirm whether waders, boots and a suitable landing net are supplied. An owned pair should still be checked for fit, condition and compatibility with the outing. Keep river-day storage small and preserve a dry change at the base. Do not describe the whole valley as freely interchangeable public access; use the selected operator or responsible local source for the actual route.')],
[gear('Reach-specific fly system','Primary presentation and target determine rod, line and leader.','Avoid a single trout setup for every objective.'),gear('Compact fly selection','Sizes and weight specified for the chosen approach.','Pattern names alone do not complete the choice.','waterproof fly fishing box','consider'),gear('Wading equipment','Confirm provision, fit and route suitability.','Bank fishing and guided wading have different requirements.','rubber sole wading boots breathable waders'),gear('Landing and release tools','Confirm suitable net and forceps with the guide.','Borrowing may remove a bulky travel item.','rubber mesh landing net fishing forceps')],
['Do not label the river glacier-fed from its color.','Do not turn a valley-wide guide into a promise for every reach.'],
['Which section and target fit our dates?','Which presentations and fly variants should we bring?','What wading equipment is included?'],['delta-clearwater-grayling','northern-patagonia-trout','mataura-trout'])

trip('lower-zambezi-tiger','Lower Zambezi tiger fishing: river conditions and a complete leader system','Lower Zambezi','Zambia','Africa','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams','Tropical floodplains'],['Tropical river'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Guided boat'],'warm',['Tigerfish'],
'A tigerfish trip is a specialist river experience, not a recycled trout or bass packing list. Start with the intended method, boat access and the operator’s complete rig.',
[('zambezi','A Zambia Tourism account describes September–November as a favorable Lower Zambezi reference, with warming, clearer conditions before the rains. It discusses fishing around current features and notes hippos and crocodiles. The article dates to 2020.')],
'The September–November reference is historical seasonal guidance, not a current river or catch report. Confirm the selected reach and conditions with the operator. Do not extend this window to every African river or every species called tigerfish.',
[('Keep access boat-led','This guide does not recommend independent bank access or wading. Ask the operator how anglers board, fish and move between locations, and follow its wildlife-aware arrangements. Do not infer safety from a photograph of calm water. A fishing plan here must include the way the customer reaches and uses the river, not just the rods in the bag.'),
('Resolve the whole terminal system','Have the operator specify the intended presentation, bite protection, connections, hooks and rod-and-line match. Do not copy a trout leader onto this trip. Avoid a universal numeric leader recommendation without the exact method and materials. Have a spare system prepared in the same configuration rather than carrying unrelated pieces that may not work together.'),
('Compare the actual fishing package','Ask which days are dedicated to fishing versus other activities, what tackle is supplied and how the boat is shared. An appealing safari package does not necessarily mean full fishing days. Keep sun coverage, rain protection and dry storage compact. Request a short list of locally useful replacements and tools before adding heavy or specialized items.')],
[gear('Tigerfish-specific leader system','Operator specifies bite protection and connections.','A generic freshwater leader is not a complete rig.'),gear('Method-matched rod and reel','Choose fly, casting or another confirmed method first.','Presentation and working load determine the setup.'),gear('Crew-approved handling tools','Confirm who handles hooks and fish.','Specialist equipment needs an agreed handling process.'),gear('Boat-day personal kit','Secure sun protection, light layers and dry bag.','Keep deck and transfer space manageable.','fishing sun gloves dry bag','consider')],
['Do not recommend wading in this river itinerary.','Do not present a 2020 seasonal article as current conditions.'],
['Which reach and current conditions support the planned week?','What complete leader and tackle system do you recommend?','How many full fishing days and anglers per boat are included?'],['rio-negro-peacock','cosmoledo-gt'])

trip('cosmoledo-gt','Cosmoledo giant trevally on fly: heavy tackle is a separate system','Cosmoledo Atoll','Seychelles','Africa','Saltwater',['Saltwater flats','Reef & bottom'],['Remote atoll'],['Fly'],['Skiff','Guided wading'],'warm',['Giant trevally'],
'An atoll GT objective should not inherit a bonefish gear list. Treat the heavy fly system, access and remote-trip logistics as a single preparation problem.',
[('cosmoledo','Blue Safari operates a Cosmoledo Atoll fly-fishing program. This establishes a specific operator lead, not Atlas inventory.'),('gt','For giant trevally, Blue Safari describes 11- or 12-weight fly rods with matched reels and a heavy tropical line/backing system. It describes target locations changing through the tidal cycle, rather than one universally best tide.')],
'No full atoll operating calendar is inferred here. Obtain the operator’s actual departure dates and an explanation of how the week’s conditions affect access. A tide stage is not a guarantee of a fish at a particular place.',
[('Audit the complete outfit','Use the operator’s current equipment list to check rod, reel, backing capacity, fly-line core, leader and connections together. A heavy rod alone does not create a robust system. Do not transfer the source’s GT recommendation to bonefish or to a different fishing method. Practice with the actual outfit and flies before departure, and ask whether rental or backup equipment can reduce purchases.'),
('Choose footwear from the route','Ask how much time is spent on a skiff and on foot, which surfaces are involved and what footwear the operator requires. Fit the boots before travel and check them with the clothing system. Keep heavy personal tackle within the actual transfer allowance. A remote atoll package needs its own baggage plan rather than a standard international airline assumption.'),
('Make secondary targets optional','A species list can tempt customers to buy multiple additional outfits. Confirm whether those targets are realistic priorities during the chosen program and whether gear is supplied. Keep the shopping list focused on the GT setup first. Use source-backed specifications for the technical system and reserve retailer searches for the exact approved variants or personal gear gaps.')],
[gear('11/12-weight GT fly system','Operator reference: matched heavy rod and reel; confirm current complete specification.','This is not an ordinary bonefish or trout outfit.'),gear('Tropical line, backing and leader','Check strength, capacity and every connection as a system.','A single strong component does not validate the rest.'),gear('Atoll footwear','Operator-approved fit and protection for the exact route.','Skiff and wading requirements may both apply.','saltwater flats wading boots'),gear('Transfer-friendly rod and bag system','Use the operator’s dimensions and weight limits.','Avoid packing equipment the transfer cannot accept.','fishing travel rod case soft duffel')],
['Do not recommend a bonefish outfit for the GT objective.','Do not infer universal tide timing or live availability.'],
['What is your current complete GT tackle specification?','Which footwear and baggage limits apply to this departure?','What backup and rental gear is available?'],['andros-bonefish','belize-permit'])

trip('mongolia-taimen','Mongolia taimen expedition: distinguish river, season and supplied gear','Fish Mongolia river programs','Mongolia','Asia','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Remote highland'],['Fly'],['River float','Guided wading'],'cool',['Taimen'],
'An expedition needs a named program and watershed, not just “Mongolia” on the packing list. Camp logistics and personal tackle provision matter as much as the fish.',
[('mongolia','Fish Mongolia distinguishes summer and autumn programs and says guests bring rods and wading equipment while flies are supplied for its program. Its notes describe warm summer days, cool evenings and possible rapid weather changes.'),('mongolia-river','The operator distinguishes programs in separate Yenisei and Amur watersheds and describes freestone canyon water for its Fish Mongolia operation.')],
'Use the actual operator program and departure date. Do not merge summer and autumn itineraries into one equipment list or assume every Mongolia river uses the same access. No live flow, catch rate or all-country season is supplied here.',
[('Select the river experience','Ask which program you are comparing, how fishing and travel days are split and where you sleep each night. A moving float camp needs different luggage organization from a fixed lodge. Discuss the group’s walking, wading and casting ability before buying equipment. The program name should remain attached to the preparation list so later changes do not silently alter the kit.'),
('Resolve supplied versus personal tackle','Confirm the actual inventory for your departure, even where the public description supplies flies but not rods and waders. Ask for rod, line and leader requirements and whether backup equipment exists. Do not apply a small-trout kit to the target solely because the water is a river. Keep spare connections and repair essentials appropriate to the approved system.'),
('Pack for transfers and changing days','Separate overnight gear from a protected day bag. Ask about baggage dimensions, drying arrangements, power and communications rather than assuming hotel facilities at camp. Use a layered clothing plan that can be adjusted, and keep the next dry layer accessible. Keep the final shopping list tied to actual missing equipment instead of every item marketed as expedition gear.')],
[gear('Operator-approved taimen outfit','Confirm rod, line and leader as a complete system.','The target and presentation differ from small-stream trout.'),gear('Waders and boots','Check actual provision, fit and drying arrangements.','Public program information indicates these may be personal equipment.','breathable fishing waders wading boots'),gear('Spare and repair kit','Prioritize connectors and essentials for the selected system.','Remote-trip redundancy should be useful, not random.'),gear('Two-part luggage system','Separate day dry bag and transfer-compliant overnight bag.','Protect accessible layers without unpacking camp luggage.','waterproof expedition duffel dry bag','consider')],
['Do not merge separate watersheds or seasonal programs.','Do not mark rods and waders provided merely because the trip includes guiding.'],
['Which river program and season does this departure use?','Which equipment is personal, supplied or rentable?','What are the transfer, drying and communication arrangements?'],['hokkaido-char','northern-patagonia-trout'])

trip('hokkaido-char','Northern Hokkaido char fishing: a different outfit from an itou trip','Northern Hokkaido','Japan','Asia','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Temperate island'],['Fly'],['Guided wading','Bank'],'cool',['White-spotted char'],
'Distinguish a char outing from a specialist itou-taimen trip. They are not interchangeable bookings merely because both appear on a Hokkaido lodge page.',
[('hokkaido','Far East Anglers describes northern Hokkaido white-spotted char trips, naming June to mid-July as a favorable period for that program and 5–6-weight fly tackle among its choices. The same page treats itou fishing as a separate heavier-tackle objective.')],
'The June–mid-July reference is operator-specific, not a calendar for all Hokkaido fish. Confirm the intended species and river program. The source also states that its char guiding does not operate during September spawning; a later date needs a different confirmed plan, not an automatically generated char page.',
[('Specify the species before the package','Tell the provider whether you want char, general trout or a specialist large-fish attempt. Ask about the actual river access and daily transfers from the lodge. Do not use a taimen photograph to sell a typical char day. A short trip should prioritize manageable transfers and usable fishing time over adding several distant species goals.'),
('Treat the published tackle as a starting point','The operator’s char outfit guidance provides a real reference, but line, fly sizes and conditions still need to be matched. Ask whether tackle is supplied or whether bringing your own suitable outfit is preferable. A heavier itou system should not be recommended by default. Keep fly boxes focused on the selected approach rather than the entire regional species list.'),
('Plan arrival and daily equipment access','Confirm the actual pickup and lodging arrangements rather than assuming a Tokyo or Sapporo arrival ends near the fishing base. Keep the first fishing day’s kit accessible during transfers. Check drying arrangements and the exact wading footwear needed. Leave questionable trophy statistics out of the decision: route, target and equipment fit are more useful.')],
[gear('Char fly outfit','Operator starting reference: 5–6-weight; finalize line and flies locally.','Do not substitute the separate itou specification.'),gear('Target-specific flies','Confirm size, weight and presentation for the dates.','Regional species names do not define a fly selection.'),gear('Wading kit','Verify rental availability, fit and exact footwear.','Avoid assuming every package includes every size.','breathable waders rubber sole boots'),gear('Compact transfer bag','Accessible tackle and dry change.','Keep day gear separate from lodge luggage.','small fishing dry bag','consider')],
['Do not mix char and itou rod specifications.','Do not publish inconsistent source measurements or promotional strike rates.'],
['Is our target char or a different fishery?','Which dates and tackle apply to that specific program?','Where do pickup and the first fishing session occur?'],['mongolia-taimen','gangwon-streams'])

trip('gangwon-streams','Gangwon mountain-stream fly fishing: a short Korea trip without overpacking','Gangwon stream programs','South Korea','Asia','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Mountain'],['Fly'],['Bank','Guided wading'],'cool',['Landlocked cherry salmon','Rainbow trout'],
'A short mountain-stream outing should be built around the actual access, time available and target—not a country-wide list of every fish an operator offers.',
[('korea','Far East Anglers describes Gangwon mountain-stream programs for landlocked cherry salmon and non-native rainbow trout and advertises tackle availability on some guided outings. Its Hokkaido and Korean experiences are separate programs.')],
'The reviewed operator overview does not establish a complete month-by-month Gangwon stream calendar. Ask which target, water and access fit the intended visit. Do not automatically add seasonal coastal or migratory opportunities to a mountain-stream booking.',
[('Use the available time well','Start with the city or accommodation where the customer will actually be staying. Compare transfer time, meeting arrangements and time on the water. For a one-day introduction, a convenient appropriate stream may provide more value than a distant name with a larger reputation. Make the level of walking and wading explicit before confirming the itinerary.'),
('Borrow before building a travel kit','Ask whether a suitable rod, reel, line and basic flies can be supplied for the selected outing. Confirm footwear separately. A traveler adding fishing to a broader visit may gain little from buying a full outfit for one day. When personal gear is preferred, obtain exact packed-length and presentation requirements rather than taking the largest rod that fits a suitcase.'),
('Keep the method and species narrow','Choose the primary trout or cherry-salmon stream objective and a small fly selection matched to the guide’s plan. Do not conflate this with the operator’s separate Hokkaido char or taimen trips. Pack a compact day bag, weather layers and a dry change, with the rest of the travel luggage left at a confirmed base.')],
[gear('Supplied or personal fly outfit','Confirm the actual model class, line and provision.','A rental may eliminate a one-day equipment purchase.'),gear('Minimal fly and leader kit','Guide-approved sizes and replacements.','A small selected box is more useful than a region-wide assortment.'),gear('Stream access footwear','Match the real walking and wading plan.','Do not assume every mountain stream demands chest waders.'),gear('Light day bag','Rain protection and dry change without hotel luggage.','Keep transfers and access manageable.','small hiking daypack dry bag','consider')],
['Do not import Hokkaido species or tackle into a Korean stream trip.','Do not promise a seasonal fish from an operator’s general regional list.'],
['Which stream and target are suitable for our day?','What is the actual pickup and transfer time?','Can rods, flies and appropriate footwear be supplied?'],['hokkaido-char','rocky-mountain-alpine'])

trip('top-end-barramundi','Top End barramundi: runoff, harbour and billabong trips are different','Northern Territory Top End','Australia','Oceania','Brackish',['Tropical floodplains','Estuaries & mangroves'],['Monsoonal'],['Lure / spinning','Fly'],['Guided boat'],'warm',['Barramundi'],
'Choose a runoff, harbour or billabong program before choosing gear. Dates alone do not tell you which Top End habitat the boat will fish.',
[('topend','Barefoot Fishing Safaris separates runoff trips, dry-season harbour fishing and build-up billabong fly programs. Its published runoff program spans late February to early June, while other programs use different periods and methods.')],
'These dates describe this operator’s programs, not fixed hydrological events. Have the guide connect the intended week to actual rain, water and access. Do not use one calendar for every Northern Territory barramundi trip.',
[('Choose the actual environment','Ask whether the selected program is fishing runoff, harbour structure, shallow sight-fishing water or a billabong. Record the named operating area once confirmed. Freshwater and brackish situations should not be merged merely because the target is barramundi. Also confirm that the operator is offering the preferred fly or conventional method on the chosen departure.'),
('Let presentation determine tackle','Ask for the intended lure weights or fly-line approach and the leader arrangement for the habitat. An outfit suitable for one presentation may not make another enjoyable. Check how much casting or lure work is involved and whether supplied equipment or a backup outfit is available. Do not buy a multi-method package until the itinerary actually includes those methods.'),
('Keep access and personal safety operator-led','Do not create a wading option from a shallow-water photograph. Ask the guide to explain how fishing positions and boat transfers are managed. The personal shopping list should focus on suitable sun and rain coverage, compact dry storage and verified footwear, while specialized equipment remains tied to the actual program.')],
[gear('Program-specific outfit','Fly or conventional tackle matched to the actual habitat.','Runoff and shallow sight-casting are different jobs.'),gear('Leader and lure/fly selection','Use the guide’s weight, size and connection requirements.','The target name alone does not establish the setup.'),gear('Boat-safe footwear and access','Operator specifies the practical movement plan.','No independent wading assumption.'),gear('Tropical boat-day kit','Sun coverage, weather shell and dry bag.','Prepare for the day rather than buying a generic expedition bundle.','fishing sun shirt waterproof dry bag','consider')],
['Do not display program dates as guaranteed rainfall or runoff.','Do not label all barramundi water freshwater.'],
['Which environment and named area does this departure fish?','What actual conditions support the program now?','Which rod, line and presentation should I bring?'],['rio-negro-peacock','texas-inshore-redfish'])

trip('tasmania-great-lake','Yingina / Great Lake trout: shore, wind lanes and trolling','Yingina / Great Lake, Tasmania','Australia','Oceania','Freshwater',['Lakes & reservoirs'],['Highland'],['Fly','Trolling','Lure / spinning'],['Bank','Guided boat'],'cool',['Brown trout','Rainbow trout'],
'A highland lake is not a slow river. Build separate plans for shore sight-fishing, boat-based fly fishing and trolling before selecting line or tackle.',
[('greatlake','Tasmania’s Inland Fisheries Service identifies Yingina / Great Lake as a Central Plateau fishery with brown and rainbow trout. It describes summer insect opportunities around late December–February, shore sight-fishing, wind-lane fishing and trolling with depth-control methods.')],
'The December–February reference is a Southern Hemisphere insect-fishing context, not the only possible way to fish the lake. Confirm the specific area and method: different bays and subwaters have different conditions and management. No live lake level or weather feed is embedded here.',
[('Choose shore or boat deliberately','For a shore day, discuss access, the amount of walking and the way you will search for fish. For a boat day, identify whether you will fly fish or troll. Those options involve different participation and tackle. Do not present a boat rental as equivalent to a guided day, or assume every shoreline is accessible under the current lake level.'),
('Treat depth control as a method choice','The agency describes trolling approaches that differ from surface-oriented fly fishing. Ask for the actual system the operator uses before buying lines, lures or accessories. A lead-core trolling setup and a dry-fly outfit should never be merged into one “trout gear” recommendation. Build the primary system first, with an explicit second method only when the itinerary supports it.'),
('Keep the weather decision separate from season','The fisheries service warns that rough conditions can develop on the lake. A highland summer booking still needs suitable layers, boat equipment and local assessment. Pack a dry change and keep the day bag organized for the selected method. Confirm practical launch or shore access before departure instead of relying solely on an old lake photograph.')],
[gear('Method-matched trout outfit','Dry-fly, lure casting or trolling chosen first.','A lake is not one tackle prescription.'),gear('Depth-control system, when trolling','Confirm line type and lure depth with the operator.','Do not buy specialized accessories for a fly-only day.'),gear('Highland weather layers','Rain protection and insulation according to forecast.','Summer dates do not remove exposure.','fishing waterproof rain jacket fleece','consider'),gear('Boat or bank equipment','Clarify access, flotation and supplied gear.','The chosen area and method determine the rest.')],
['Do not turn December–February hatches into a Northern Hemisphere summer rule.','Do not treat an open lake label as identical conditions in every bay.'],
['Will we fish from shore, fly fish from a boat or troll?','What are the current lake level and access conditions?','Which line and depth-control system is actually used?'],['rocky-mountain-alpine','northern-patagonia-trout'])

trip('mataura-trout','Mataura River trout planning: choose a reach and a presentation','Mataura River, Southland','New Zealand','Oceania','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Temperate lowland'],['Fly'],['Bank','Guided wading'],'cool',['Trout'],
'Build a Southland river day around access, presentation and the chosen reach. A trout-river reputation is not a complete plan for a visiting angler.',
[('nz-rivers','Tourism New Zealand describes the Mataura between the Garston area and downstream of Gore as a gently graded river with tributaries and notable hatches. That broad description does not establish access or conditions at every reach.')],
'The reviewed article does not supply a current hatch report or a universal monthly calendar. Confirm the intended section and current conditions. Use Southern Hemisphere dates explicitly rather than importing a Montana seasonal template.',
[('Select the day’s objective','Ask whether the plan emphasizes learning, searching a reach or targeting a particular presentation opportunity. State how far the group wants to walk and whether wading is acceptable. Use a guide or current local access resource to identify the actual starting point; a long river named in an article is not a single usable map pin.'),
('Build a coherent primary method','Discuss dry-fly and subsurface alternatives for the chosen dates without assuming a surface hatch will occur on demand. Match the rod, fly line, leader and actual fly sizes. Avoid buying a large country-wide selection that leaves the important sizes unspecified. A small replenishment purchase from a local shop can remain part of the plan.'),
('Keep travel and river-day gear separate','Confirm whether the operator collects customers from their accommodation and whether tackle and waders are included. Keep a compact day bag with weather layers and a separate dry change. Record what equipment was useful after the day so a later personal purchase follows actual experience rather than the excitement of booking the trip.')],
[gear('Presentation-specific fly outfit','Line, leader and fly sizes matched together.','A generic trout label does not finish the specification.'),gear('Small selected fly box','Primary approach plus a clear subsurface or surface alternative.','Avoid overbuying patterns without useful sizes.','compact waterproof fly fishing box','consider'),gear('Confirmed wading system','Appropriate fit and provision for the actual reach.','Do not assume all itineraries include rental sizes.'),gear('Layered day kit','Rain layer and dry change with practical storage.','Keep the river bag separate from travel luggage.','small waterproof fly fishing pack','consider')],
['Do not turn a regional river overview into live hatch intelligence.','Do not copy Northern Hemisphere month labels.'],
['Which reach and presentation fit the intended day?','What access and walking does the trip involve?','Which tackle and wading equipment are provided?'],['eg linton-river'.replace(' ',''),'northern-patagonia-trout','soca-fly-fishing'])

trip('eglinton-river','Eglinton River fishing: a Fiordland valley day, not an alpine-lake template','Eglinton River, Fiordland','New Zealand','Oceania','Freshwater',['Rivers & streams'],['Mountain valley','Lake-fed'],['Fly','Lure / spinning'],['Bank','Guided wading'],'cool',['Brown trout','Rainbow trout'],
'Distinguish a valley river from a hike-in mountain lake. The same scenic region can contain very different access, timing and equipment requirements.',
[('nz-rivers','Tourism New Zealand describes the Eglinton flowing from Lake Gunn toward Lake Te Anau through a broad meadow and identifies brown and rainbow trout. It suggests Te Anau as a regional base.')],
'This overview establishes the river setting, not a current water-level report, access map or monthly fishing guarantee. Confirm a specific reach and route for the selected date, with a practical alternative if conditions make the plan unsuitable.',
[('Attach the fishing to the actual travel day','Decide whether this is a dedicated guided day or fishing added to a broader Fiordland itinerary. Preserve enough time for transfers, access and the return journey. Do not confuse a river visible from a scenic route with an immediately usable fishing location. Confirm where the guide meets you and which equipment can stay at the accommodation.'),
('Choose method without importing unrelated gear','Ask whether a fly or lure presentation fits the intended reach and the customer’s experience. Match the setup to that presentation. A backpacking alpine-lake rod chosen purely for packed length may not be the best comparison for a vehicle-supported river day. Equally, a large boat bag makes little sense for a walking session.'),
('Use access-specific personal equipment','Confirm wading depth expectations, footwear and gear provision. Keep the dry change separate and protect only what needs waterproof storage, avoiding unnecessary bulk. Build a compact list around the chosen method and local recommendation. Make time for observation and instruction instead of treating every minute off the road as an obligation to cast.')],
[gear('Selected river outfit','Choose fly or lure and actual presentation weights.','Valley scenery does not decide the rod.'),gear('Compact tackle selection','Specific flies or lures for the chosen method.','Do not carry all possible trout methods by default.'),gear('Access-matched footwear','Walking and wading plan confirmed before purchase.','Hike-in lake assumptions do not automatically apply.'),gear('Dry change and day storage','Keep river gear separate from the rest of the journey.','Prepare for a travel day that continues after fishing.','compact waterproof day bag','consider')],
['Do not classify every Fiordland fishing trip as a remote alpine-lake hike.','Do not mistake a broad river description for an access map.'],
['What named reach and meeting point are planned?','Does the day fit the rest of our travel itinerary?','Which method, footwear and supplied gear apply?'],['mataura-trout','rocky-mountain-alpine'])

source('estuary','NOAA — What is an estuary?','https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/estuary.html')
source('reading-water','Orvis Learning Center — Reading the water','https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/video-lessons/the-basics-of-fly-fishing/chapter-eight-reading-water','manufacturer-education','Text introduction and lesson outline inspected; not a claim that every embedded video was watched.')
source('stillwater','Orvis Learning Center — Stillwater trout','https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/video-lessons/the-basics-of-fly-fishing/chapter-nine-stillwater-trout','manufacturer-education','Text introduction and lesson outline inspected.')
source('flats-learning','Orvis Learning Center — Saltwater inshore flats','https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/video-lessons/the-basics-of-fly-fishing/chapter-11-fly-fishing-saltwater-flats','manufacturer-education','Text introduction and lesson outline inspected.')
source('equipment-learning','Orvis Learning Center — Equipment','https://howtoflyfish.orvis.com/choosing-equipment/equipment-videos','manufacturer-education','Educational summaries inspected; product marketing is not independent product testing.')

HABITATS=[]
def habitat(id,name,deck,source_id,claim,sections,checklist):
    HABITATS.append({'id':id,'name':name,'title':name+' fishing guide: understand the water before the gear','summary':deck,'evidence':[{'source':source_id,'claim':claim}],'sections':[{'title':a,'text':b} for a,b in sections],'checklist':checklist,'review':{'status':'source-checked','checked':DATE,'fieldTested':False,'independentExpertReviewed':False}})

habitat('rivers-streams','Rivers & streams','A current-driven fishery must be narrowed by target, reach, presentation and access—not just country.',
'reading-water','Orvis explains trout location in terms of current, food and protection and treats approaching fish and reading different river features as separate skills.',
[('Read a small piece of water first','For a trout outing, begin by observing rather than casting across every visible current. Identify a manageable fishing position and the presentation you want to make. Think about where the fly or lure enters, how it travels and how you can recover it. Work a small area deliberately before moving. This is an introductory approach, not a claim that every river species uses identical habitat.'),
('Separate method from access','A river can be fished from a bank, by wading or from a boat. The method may be fly, lure, bait or another locally appropriate approach. Choose these independently. Waders do not belong in every river list, and a drift-boat booking does not automatically include every personal item. In wildlife-sensitive or hazardous settings, keep access within the actual guide plan rather than creating an independent wading option.'),
('Build one functional setup','Write down the presentation sizes or weights and the conditions they must work in. Use that information to choose a rod, reel, line and terminal system together. A small-stream trout day and a specialist tropical predator trip are not the same outfit with different lures. If a second approach is needed, describe exactly what changes instead of adding random tackle.'),
('Use conditions to choose the day','An annual season helps select a trip, but the reach still needs current assessment. Identify who supplies the latest access and water information and what conditions change the plan. Keep those decisions separate from a catch guarantee. Save the final setup and what you learned afterward so the next visit starts from experience rather than another generic shopping list.')],
['Named river and reach','Target and primary presentation','Bank, guided wading or boat access','Matched tackle and handling plan','Current-condition check and alternative'])

habitat('spring-fed','Spring-fed streams','Groundwater influence is a hydrological distinction, not a promise that every spring-fed stream behaves the same.',
'clearwater','The Delta Clearwater example is cold, clear and spring-fed; ADF&G describes dry flies, nymphs and small lures there. Its access notes also show that a small stream is not necessarily easy to fish from shore.',
[('Avoid the clear-water shortcut','Clear water does not automatically mean easy fishing, safe wading or abundant public access. Choose the named stream and target first. Observe how much cover exists and what presentation space is available. For a visiting angler, a short local introduction can help turn visual observations into a practical fishing approach without buying a large new kit.'),
('Keep the presentation controlled','For a fly plan, select a small set of locally useful dry and subsurface options and match their sizes to the leader and line system. Do not automatically reduce every component to the lightest available. The aim is a manageable presentation that also supports appropriate fish handling. For spinning, start with the actual useful lure weights rather than an undefined ultralight label.'),
('Plan access independently','A spring creek may involve a guided beat, a limited bank area or a float rather than an unrestricted walk along the river. Ask where the customer will actually stand or sit and how the day begins and ends. Choose footwear only after those answers. Protect a dry change without packing a complete expedition bag for a short session.'),
('Do not reuse a neighboring calendar','The named spring-fed water and its target deserve their own evidence. A report for a larger nearby glacial river may not describe the same clarity or access conditions. Retain the original observation date and avoid displaying an old report as a current forecast. Use the source-linked local profiles below for concrete examples rather than treating the category as one worldwide fishery.')],
['Evidence that the stream is spring-fed','Actual target and presentation','Public or operator-arranged access','Small, selected fly or lure range','Separate water and weather checks'])

habitat('glacier-influenced','Glacier-influenced rivers','Glacial influence, ice fishing and travel on a glacier are three different concepts. Only the first describes a river’s water source.',
'tanana-report','A June 26, 2026 ADF&G observation linked rising Tanana water to warming and glacial melt. It is a dated example of changing water, not a fixed calendar or live forecast.',
[('Name the hydrological situation','Identify whether the planned fishing is on a glacier-influenced mainstem, a tributary, a lake outlet or a clear-water pocket within the drainage. Do not infer hydrology from blue water or mountain scenery. The actual glacier may be far from the fishing. This guide provides no instructions for traveling on a glacier or fishing beside unstable ice.'),
('Do not confuse visibility with access','A cloudy-looking mainstem and a nearby clear tributary can require different approaches. Neither appearance proves wading safety or fish presence. Select an established fishery and an appropriate guide or access resource, then define the target and method. The river’s classification alone cannot select a rod or lure.'),
('Preserve the date on observations','A report should identify its observation date, water and reach. A June melt observation must not become a permanent “conditions now” banner. Keep seasonal background, recent observations and forecasts in different fields. Where those data are missing, the trip can still offer preparation guidance without inventing a precise visibility or flow forecast.'),
('Build an adaptable itinerary','Ask which named alternative remains useful if the selected water is unsuitable. Keep transport, launch and return plans explicit. Pack equipment for the agreed targets rather than every species in the drainage. Personal dry storage, accessible layers and a clear communications plan may add more value than another generic lure assortment.')],
['Mainstem versus tributary identified','Hydrology supported by a source','Observation dates visible','No glacier-travel assumptions','Named-water tackle and contingency'])

habitat('lakes-reservoirs','Lakes & reservoirs','Stillwater fishing is not river fishing without current. Depth, presentation, access and the target need their own plan.',
'stillwater','Orvis’s stillwater instruction separates finding trout, structure, line selection, presentation and retrieves from stream techniques.',
[('Choose the fishing layer and access','Start with the actual target, season and access method: bank, guided boat or another appropriate craft. Ask how the local approach reaches fish and keeps the presentation in a useful zone. Avoid buying accessories before deciding whether you will cast, retrieve, suspend a presentation or troll. A single lake may support several very different fishing days.'),
('Treat line choice as a tool','For a fly trip, the presentation and intended depth influence the line system. For trolling or casting, the local depth-control approach is a separate equipment decision. Do not describe all of these as one trout combo. Ask for an explanation of how the chosen system is used and select a small number of compatible components instead of accumulating alternatives you cannot deploy.'),
('Check actual shore and launch access','A waterbody outline on a map does not establish a usable bank or boat launch at the current level. Confirm the relevant entry point and the day’s operating conditions. Keep navigation, flotation and boat handling distinct from the fishing recommendation. A calm photograph does not establish safe conditions for an inexperienced boat user.'),
('Compare methods by customer value','A walking shore session, a boat-based fly day and trolling can offer very different levels of physical participation. Match the customer’s preferred pace and skill to the activity. A guide who supplies specialized tackle may remove the need for a large first-time purchase. Keep personal weather protection and dry storage common across methods while changing only the technical kit that is actually required.')],
['Target and method','Bank or boat plan','Depth and presentation system','Actual access at current water level','Weather and equipment provision'])

habitat('alpine-lakes','Alpine lakes','A mountain setting adds an access and carrying problem. Fish presence must be established before the hike.',
'rocky','Rocky Mountain National Park’s guidance cautions that many high-altitude lakes lack reproducing fish populations. That makes lake-specific verification essential rather than optional.',
[('Make the hike and fishing fit each other','Identify a named fishery and an actual route. Compare approach distance, ascent, return time and the group’s ability before selecting equipment. Decide how much of the day is for fishing. A hike with optional casting should not carry the same tackle load as a dedicated multi-day angling expedition.'),
('Reduce bulk without losing the basics','Select one working outfit, a small presentation range and protected spare leader or line components. A compact travel rod should still be rated for what it will cast. Limit duplicates before removing essential outdoor equipment. Consider whether a packable net is useful for the intended fish and method rather than adding one solely because a shopping template includes it.'),
('Keep snow and water conditions local','Do not assign a universal ice-off month to every elevation or latitude. Identify a current information source for the selected trail and lake. A summer date does not prove the approach is open or that the fishing objective is appropriate. If current information cannot support the trip, choose a better-established alternative instead of letting a published packing list create false confidence.'),
('Do not assume wading','Bank fishing may avoid a bulky wading purchase entirely. The approach footwear, shoreline and intended fishing position should guide the decision. Set a return time, organize weather protection and preserve a dry layer. The shopping list should help carry less while still supporting the whole day, not only the moments spent fishing.')],
['Fishery verified at named lake','Approach and current access checked','Return-time plan','Packable compatible outfit','No automatic waders purchase'])

habitat('estuaries-mangroves','Estuaries & mangroves','Separate the water’s salinity, its cover and the chosen access. “Inshore” is too broad to finish the equipment decision.',
'estuary','NOAA describes typical coastal estuaries as places where freshwater and seawater mix, while also noting freshwater estuary systems. The name alone therefore does not establish one uniform salinity or habitat.',
[('Name the actual cover','A mangrove edge, grass flat, oyster area and channel are different situations within a broad coastal trip. Pick the target and method for the named area, not for the entire country. Ask what abrasion, casting space and presentation are relevant. Do not substitute a freshwater-pond tackle list just because the boat begins in a river.'),
('Connect tides to the local route','Use a relevant tide source and the guide’s explanation of access and fishing windows, rather than a universal rule that one tide stage is always best. Weather and local water movement still need assessment. Keep any daily recommendation linked to the specific area, date and target; do not infer precise feeding times from a month alone.'),
('Choose access before footwear','A boat, bank and guided-wading trip create different personal equipment requirements. Do not generate wading options where local hazards make that inappropriate. Ask the operator about the actual route and movement between fishing positions. Keep boat storage compact and separate technical gear that may already be provided.'),
('Build the tackle against the habitat','Request a complete rod, reel, line and leader arrangement with the chosen presentation. Abrasion requirements and casting loads matter more than a generic inshore label. Borrow or rent a suitable outfit for an introductory day when available. Buy the missing personal items first, then any technical replacements the operator explicitly recommends.')],
['Salinity and habitat identified','Primary target and cover','Local tide and access context','No automatic wading','Matched abrasion and presentation needs'])

habitat('saltwater-flats','Saltwater flats','A shallow-water category spanning different targets, substrates and techniques. It is not one universal bonefish kit.',
'flats-learning','Orvis separates habitat, tides, spotting, guide communication, fly selection and presentation in its flats instruction. Fly and spinning approaches are also treated separately.',
[('Choose one primary fish','A broad list of bonefish, permit, tarpon or trevally should not become an automatic requirement to buy every outfit. Set the customer’s main objective and what alternatives would still make an enjoyable day. Ask how difficult the chosen presentation is for their current skill and whether instruction is part of the booking.'),
('Practice the actual task','Use the outfit intended for the trip, with a practice arrangement appropriate to the setting. Clarify the casting distances and wind conditions the guide expects and work on the relevant presentation. Organize line and equipment so the first cast is not obstructed. More expensive equipment is not a substitute for understanding the task.'),
('Select a complete target system','Rod, reel, line, backing, leader, connections and fly need to work together. Specialist GT and ordinary bonefish choices should not be mixed simply because both are on flats. Ask for exact fly sizes and weighting rather than buying a collection by pattern name alone. Keep a second outfit conditional on an actual planned secondary target.'),
('Respect the surface underfoot','Ask whether the day is skiff-only or includes guided wading and which footwear suits the real substrate. A sandy-flat photograph does not describe every atoll route. Keep personal sun and rain layers compact, secure glasses and loose items, and confirm the operator’s current baggage and equipment arrangements before purchasing travel-specific products.')],
['Primary target','Fly or conventional method','Complete compatible system','Skiff versus guided wading','Local tide, wind and substrate context'])

habitat('surf-beaches','Surf & beaches','A shore session needs a target, whole-rig casting load and practical equipment carry—not just a long rod.',
'hatteras','Cape Hatteras visitor information distinguishes surf and pier fishing from sound and offshore trips. Its seasonal overview is broad and should not be read as a universal species calendar.',
[('Choose lure casting or bait presentation','A lure-casting session and a bait-and-sinker session are different jobs. Specify the target and intended presentation, then check the entire casting load against the rod. Do not choose a rod by length alone. A practical outfit the angler can control is preferable to a large purchase made only to match a surf-fishing image.'),
('Keep the working kit small','Carry the tackle needed for the next session in a closed box and keep replenishment separate when possible. Decide how the rod will be supported and how reels stay out of the sand. Consider a cart only after checking the actual route, carry distance and storage. A cart is optional equipment, not proof that a beach is accessible.'),
('Use local conditions, not a generic tide slogan','Ask about the target, surf and useful presentation for the selected day. A tide prediction and a catch forecast are not the same thing. Choose a suitable shore position and do not equate a public beach with safe wading. Make changing weather a reason to reassess rather than to use heavier gear without understanding the conditions.'),
('Compare a guided introduction with more equipment','For a first surf trip, local instruction can answer casting, rigging and positioning questions that a shopping basket cannot. Start with a coherent setup and a small number of replacement components. Keep personal sun protection, weather layers and drinking-water arrangements in the list alongside the fishing tackle.')],
['Target and presentation','Total casting load','Carry distance and sand management','Actual surf and access assessment','Replacement rigs rather than random tackle'])

habitat('reef-bottom','Reef & bottom','Bottom-oriented fishing needs depth, current, structure and the crew’s method before a useful tackle recommendation exists.',
'halibut','The Yakutat halibut reference includes both bait and large jigs. That difference illustrates why even one bottom-fishing target can require different techniques and equipment choices.',
[('Specify what the angler does','Ask whether the trip uses stationary bait, drifting, active jigging or another crew-selected approach. Clarify how much of the day the customer holds and works a rod and whether equipment support is used. A beginner-friendly reef outing and a demanding deep-water session may share a broad label but are not the same customer experience.'),
('Choose the load as a system','Depth, current and the presentation determine useful tackle. Ask the skipper for the expected weight range and compatible line and reel capacity. Do not substitute a stronger rod while ignoring the rest of the rig. Avoid recommending heavy sinkers or jigs from destination alone, and confirm which items are already supplied.'),
('Separate local targets','A tropical reef fish list does not belong on a subarctic halibut page. Keep species and seasonal guidance tied to the actual fishery. Do not guarantee catch or retention from a photograph. Fish handling and specialized tools should follow the crew’s process rather than a consumer checklist that encourages untrained improvisation.'),
('Pack the boat day, not a tackle warehouse','Use approved deck footwear and compact personal storage. Confirm the boat’s shelter, facilities and drinking-water provision. Buy personal gaps first and transport technical equipment only when the operator requests or approves it. Save the actual successful setup afterward so the next purchase is grounded in use.')],
['Depth and current range','Bait, drift or jig method','Matched tackle and provided inventory','Realistic physical participation','Crew-led handling and boat-day kit'])

habitat('offshore','Offshore','Offshore is a location class. Trolling, casting, jigging and deep bottom fishing remain distinct methods within it.',
'keys-world','The Florida Keys visitor bureau differentiates offshore and reef/wreck experiences and names different fishing opportunities. Those categories should remain visible when comparing trips.',
[('Book the method, not only the species photo','Ask what the trip is designed to do and what each angler actually controls. A trolling day can have a different rhythm from a casting or jigging day. Compare active fishing time, travel to grounds, group size and facilities. A headline duration is not enough to determine the value of the outing.'),
('Prefer a crew-matched first setup','For an introductory charter, confirm supplied equipment before buying specialized rods and reels. The complete system should match the target and technique. A large spinning reel is not automatically suitable for trolling, and a heavy bottom rod is not a default casting outfit. Personal tackle should add a clear benefit rather than duplicate the boat inventory.'),
('Keep the weather alternative explicit','Ask how the operator handles unsuitable conditions and whether an alternate route or method is offered. That alternative should be presented as a different experience where appropriate. Do not promise that a short inshore outing replaces an offshore objective without customer agreement. Keep quoted inclusions, decisions and payment status in the same trip plan.'),
('Prepare for time aboard','Ask about seating, shade, facilities and the expected length of the outing. Pack appropriate deck footwear and accessible personal layers. Explain any limitations on sustained physical effort directly to the operator. A well-matched day is more valuable than expensive equipment purchased for an activity the customer will not enjoy.')],
['Primary target and method','Transit versus fishing time','Crew-supplied equipment','Weather alternative defined','Group participation and comfort'])

habitat('ice-fishing','Ice fishing','Fishing through lake ice is not the same as fishing glacier-fed water. Local surface assessment is part of the outing.',
'finland','The Iso-Virmas operator listing describes a guided ice-fishing experience with instruction. Its advertised season does not establish safe ice for independent access.',
[('Start with a locally assessed trip','Choose a provider that can assess the actual location and day rather than treating a month as permission to enter a frozen lake. Ask what conditions cause a change or cancellation. This category guide intentionally contains no universal ice-thickness assurance. The preparation tool should preserve unknown access as unresolved, not turn it green when the customer buys equipment.'),
('Rent technical equipment for an introduction','Ask whether rods, jigs or bait, an auger, seating and other local equipment are supplied. An introductory session can reveal which purchases would actually be useful later. Do not add powered equipment just because a catalog has it. The customer’s first goal is a comfortable, supervised experience, not ownership of every tool.'),
('Choose clothing for the planned activity','The duration and amount of movement should inform the clothing discussion. Confirm rental sizes and whether boots or outer garments are part of the package. Keep a spare dry pair of gloves and ask about warm-up facilities. Clothing used during active exercise should not be assumed sufficient for a largely stationary fishing session.'),
('Keep the day simple','Establish meeting point, access, session length and return arrangements. Use a manageable personal bag rather than separate loose items. Confirm the intended fish and presentation locally. Afterward, record what was supplied and what you used so future purchases are specific rather than driven by a generic winter-fishing checklist.')],
['Local surface and route assessment','Confirmed operating status','Supplied tools and tackle','Clothing fit and warm-up options','No calendar-based safety assumptions'])

habitat('tropical-floodplains','Tropical floodplains','Basin and water phase matter. A single “tropical river” calendar cannot describe every tributary, floodplain or target.',
'amazon-planner','The Rio Negro operator’s preparation library treats water levels as a separate planning subject from tackle and travel. That is the model used here: basin first, then the actual operating period.',
[('Name the basin before the month','Ask for the named river system and operating area, not just the country or “Amazon.” Clarify whether the fishing uses a fixed lodge, moving base or daily transfers. The same travel week can describe different practical conditions across different waters; do not import another basin’s calendar without evidence.'),
('Link presentation to the habitat','Choose the target and intended presentation before specifying rod and line. Casting larger predator lures, working flies and fishing other methods require different systems. Ask for exact weights, hook and connection requirements, and how much repetitive casting is involved. Select a compact set of approved alternatives rather than a large collection of unverified local lookalikes.'),
('Make access and logistics explicit','Keep wildlife-aware access with the operator; do not generate independent wading simply because the water is shallow. Confirm transfers, baggage allowance, communications and the way overnight gear is moved. A moving fishing base and a fixed lodge have different packing needs, even when both market the same target.'),
('Buy only the missing part','Some packages provide extensive equipment while others expect personal tackle. Confirm the actual departure’s inventory, including replacements and tools. Put personal weather coverage and dry storage ahead of speculative specialist purchases. Keep any claims about insects, easy fishing or constant access out of the guide unless well-supported for the specific situation.')],
['Named basin and operating area','Documented seasonal context','Exact target and presentation','Transfer and baggage plan','Equipment provision confirmed'])

# Source-linked technique notes add concrete information without inventing universal ratings.
DETAILS={
'situk-coho-river':('situk','ADF&G’s useful presentation families include streamer/leech flies, jigs, spinners and spoons. Its coho guidance includes bright fly and lure options. Choose one of those families, then obtain the useful size and weight for the intended reach; do not assume a mixed salmon kit contains the right variants.'),
'yakutat-halibut':('halibut','The agency lists herring, squid and octopus as bait examples, plus large squid jigs and spoons. These are documented method options, not a recommendation to bring bait through your entire journey. Have the skipper supply or approve the exact bait, rig and presentation weight.'),
'delta-clearwater-grayling':('clearwater','For grayling, ADF&G suggests matching aquatic insects with dry flies and switching to nymphs when surface fishing is less productive; small spoons, spinners and jigs are its conventional options. Pools and slack eddies are highlighted. This is a concrete starting approach, not a current hatch report.'),
'cosmoledo-gt':('gt','The operator’s published heavy system includes approximately 300 yards of 80-pound braid backing and a tropical floating fly line with an 80-pound core. Confirm capacity with the exact reel and follow the operator’s current leader/connection instructions. These details apply to its GT fly approach, not all flats fishing.'),
'finland-ice-fishing':('finland','The specific listing describes jig-style hole fishing using a red worm and mentions perch. It lists December–March as its intended experience season. Actual operation still depends on local conditions; the seasonal listing is not an assurance of safe ice.'),
'keys-flats':('flats-learning','Orvis’s instructional sequence starts with habitat and tides, then spotting, guide communication and presentation. Prepare for those tasks: agree how the guide calls direction and distance, keep the line clear, and practice placing the intended presentation rather than only casting as far as possible.'),
'cape-hatteras-surf':('hatteras','The park distinguishes surf, piers, sounds and offshore waters. Use those as separate route and equipment choices. Its broad spring/fall guidance is not a reason to skip a target-specific check or to use a surf rig on a charter.'),
}
for t in TRIPS:
    if t['id'] in DETAILS:
        s,txt=DETAILS[t['id']]
        t['techniqueNote']={'source':s,'text':txt}
    t['seasonWindows']=[]
WINDOWS={
'yakutat-halibut':([4,5,6,7,8,9],'Broad agency reference; April means late April','halibut'),
'delta-clearwater-grayling':([5,6,7,8,9],'Grayling reference only','clearwater'),
'cape-hatteras-surf':([3,4,5,9,10,11],'Broad spring/fall editorial month mapping, not a species calendar','hatteras'),
'norway-skrei':([1,2,3],'Broad winter skrei reference','norway'),
'finland-ice-fishing':([12,1,2,3],'Operator-listed intended season; actual ice assessment required','finland'),
'lower-zambezi-tiger':([9,10,11],'Historical seasonal guidance from 2020','zambezi'),
'hokkaido-char':([6,7],'Operator char reference; July means through mid-July','hokkaido'),
'top-end-barramundi':([2,3,4,5,6],'Operator runoff program; late February through early June only','topend'),
'tasmania-great-lake':([12,1,2],'Summer insect opportunity; December means late December','greatlake'),
'northern-patagonia-trout':([12,1],'Specific lake dragonfly reference, not entire trout season','patagonia')
}
for t in TRIPS:
    if t['id'] in WINDOWS:
        months,label,s=WINDOWS[t['id']]
        t['seasonWindows']=[dict(months=months,label=label,source=s)]
    t['sourceIds']=list(dict.fromkeys([e['source'] for e in t['evidence']]+([t['techniqueNote']['source']] if t.get('techniqueNote') else [])))
    t['sectionWordCount']=sum(len(s['text'].split()) for s in t['sections'])
    t['articleWordCount']=len((' '.join([t['summary'],t['season']]+[e['claim'] for e in t['evidence']]+[s['text'] for s in t['sections']]+[g['specification']+' '+g['why'] for g in t['kit']]+t['mistakes']+t['questions'])).split())

# Explicit editorial issue decisions made during research. These are not hidden from users.
ISSUES=[
{'severity':'excluded','source':'hokkaido','issue':'Source paired 40 inches with 60 cm. Those measurements are inconsistent.','decision':'Omitted the numeric fish-size claim and promotional strike-frequency estimate.'},
{'severity':'excluded','source':'gt','issue':'The operator species index had duplicated text in an unrelated species description.','decision':'Used the dedicated giant-trevally page; did not copy the index text.'},
{'severity':'excluded','source':'clearwater','issue':'Some agency structured begin/end date strings were malformed.','decision':'Used the readable seasonal prose, not broken date tokens.'},
{'severity':'qualified','source':'tanana-report','issue':'A seasonal river observation is dated June 26, 2026.','decision':'Retained observation date and historical status; no live-conditions claim.'},
{'severity':'qualified','source':'zambezi','issue':'Local seasonal story dates to 2020.','decision':'Label historical seasonal background; keep current-condition checks unresolved.'},
{'severity':'excluded','source':'amazon','issue':'Promotional copy generalized insect conditions and fishing outcomes.','decision':'Did not use those assertions as trip facts.'},
{'severity':'qualified','source':'greatlake','issue':'Different named subwaters have distinct access/management context.','decision':'No lake-wide inference from one bay or one listed opening.'},
]
BACKLOG=[
('North America','Canada','Great Lakes trolling, Canadian Shield pike/walleye and Pacific salmon','Separate lake, species, season and boat methods; primary local sources needed.'),
('North America','United States','Bass reservoirs, tailwaters, urban ponds and kayak fisheries','Add named waters, habitat/season evidence and method-specific gear.'),
('South America','Chile','Patagonian lakes, sea-run trout and remote river floats','Do not reuse Argentina seasonal or access statements.'),
('Europe','United Kingdom and Ireland','Coarse fishing, carp, chalkstreams and Atlantic salmon','Species/method-specific editorial review needed; not a generic trout expansion.'),
('Europe','Iceland','Salmon rivers, Arctic char and sea fishing','Glacier geography does not establish which waters are fishable.'),
('Africa','South Africa and Namibia','Rock shore, surf and offshore fisheries','Local surf/rock access and target-specific tackle sources required.'),
('Asia','Maldives and Oman','Atoll casting, jigging, tuna and trevally','Monsoon, specific atoll and presentation evidence required.'),
('Asia','Thailand and India','Native river fisheries and stocked venues','Distinguish managed ponds from native ecosystems and wild targets.'),
('Oceania','Australia','Great Barrier Reef, temperate estuaries and impoundment bass','Keep reef, offshore and freshwater methods separate.'),
('Oceania','New Zealand','Lake trolling, sea-run salmon and saltwater kingfish','Add dedicated local source and equipment chains.'),
('Pacific','Kiribati and Papua New Guinea','Flats, reef casting and tropical river predators','Remote travel, access and species verification needed before publication.'),
]

CORPUS={'edition':'002','reviewed':DATE,'sources':SOURCES,'trips':TRIPS,'habitats':HABITATS,'issues':ISSUES,'backlog':[dict(region=r,country=c,topic=t,needed=n,status='not-published') for r,c,t,n in BACKLOG]}
if __name__=='__main__':
    (ROOT/'src/world.json').write_text(json.dumps(CORPUS,ensure_ascii=False,indent=2))
    print(f'{len(TRIPS)} trip guides, {len(HABITATS)} environment guides, {len(SOURCES)} sources')
